ues?\nprint(\"empty clubs:\", sum(1 for row in rows if not row[idx['Club']].strip()))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788256549979,"end":1788256549995}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good data exploration. Key findings:\n\n1. **Team name variants**: \n   - Brasileirão: \"Palmeiras-SP\" (with state suffix), some without accents (\"America-MG\", \"Avai-SC\", \"Atletico-MG\" — unaccented!) \n   - Historical: \"América-MG\", \"Avaí\", \"Atlético-MG\" (accented) — so accents differ between files for the same team.\n   - Copa: \"América - MG\" (with spaces around dash), \"A.b.c. - RN\" (dotted lowercase!), \"ABC - RN\" and \"Abc - RN\" — same team with different case.\n   - BR-Football: \"4 de Julho EC\", \"Aguia Negra-MS\" — suffixes like \"EC\", \"FC\" attached.\n   - Libertadores: international teams, \"Athletico\" (no suffix), \"América-MG\" mixed.\n   \n2. **Goals**: 'NA' (bras 164, copa 32), '-' (lib 4) — need to handle missing scores.\n\n3. **Dates**: \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" (ISO with time), \"2023-09-24\" (ISO date only), \"29/03/2003\" (BR format). BRF has separate time column.\n\n4. **FIFA data**: first column is unnamed ('\\ufeff' = BOM). Need encoding='utf-8-sig' or handle BOM.\n\nNormalization design:\n- Strip accents (unicodedata NFD → ASCII), lowercase, remove punctuation.\n- Remove state suffixes: trailing \"-SP\", \" - RJ\" patterns (2-letter UF codes), remove club suffix words like \"EC\", \"FC\", \"Sport Club\", \"Esporte Clube\"? Careful: \"Sport\" is a team (Sport-PE / Sport Recife)! So can't blindly remove \"sport\". Remove common suffix tokens only when trailing: EC, FC, AC? Hmm \"AC\" could clash. Let me be careful:\n  - Remove trailing state codes (list of 27 Brazilian UF abbreviations).\n  - Remove trailing \"EC\", \"FC\", \"SC\"? \"SC\" is also Santa Catarina state code... conflict: \"Aguia Negra-MS\" has -MS. But \"SC\" as trailing token without dash... risky. Keep it simple: handle dash-suffixed UF, \" - UF\" spaced form, and trailing club-designator words (\"EC\", \"FC\", \"FBC\"?) only for a small safe set.\n  - Handle dotted forms: \"A.b.c.\" → \"abc\". Remove dots first.\n  - Known aliases mapping: e.g., \"atletico-mg\" in unaccented files vs \"Atlético-MG\" — accent stripping handles that. \"Athletico-PR\" vs \"Atlético-PR\"? Actually these are the same club (Athletico Paranaense was renamed from Atlético Paranaense in 2019). Hmm — in historico data, 2019 rows might say \"Athletico-PR\" and earlier \"Atlético-PR\". Those normalize to \"athletico-pr\" vs \"atletico-pr\". For matching \"Athletico-PR\" queries we want both. I can add an alias table for known same-club variants: athletico/atletico paranaense.\n  \n  But careful not to over-engineer. The core requirement: normalize for consistent matching across files. Accent-insensitive + case-insensitive + UF-suffix removal + punctuation removal handles most. Add a small manual alias table for known equivalences:\n  - \"atletico-pr\" == \"athletico-pr\"\n  - \"america-rn\" vs \"abc\"? no.\n  - \"atletico-go\" vs \"atlético-go\" — accent handles.\n  \n  Also match should be fuzzy-tolerant: query \"Corinthians\" should match \"Corinthians-SP\" and \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\". Approach: exact normalized match first; fallback to prefix/containment matching; also a canonical registry that maps every observed variant to a canonical display name.\n\n- Matching strategy: build a registry of all team variants from all files → canonical key. When user queries \"Palmeiras\", normalize to \"palmeiras\", look up registry: exact key match; else keys that start with query or contain it as a whole word. Return matches. For disambiguation (e.g., \"América\" could be América-MG, América-RN, América-MEX...), exact normalized match preferred; if ambiguous, tools return all candidates and the LLM resolves.\n\n4. **Date parsing**: try formats: \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\", \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\". For BRF combine date+time.\n\n5. **FIFA CSV BOM**: use utf-8-sig.\n\nArchitecture:\n\n```\nbrsoccer/\n  __init__.py\n  models.py      # Match, Player dataclasses\n  normalize.py   # team-name normalization + TeamRegistry\n  dates.py       # multi-format date parsing\n  data.py        # CSV loaders -> list[Match], list[Player]; SoccerData container\n  queries.py     # query engine functions (pure, no MCP dependency)\n  formatting.py  # human-friendly formatted answers\n  server.py      # MCP server wiring (FastMCP)\nserver.py        # entry point script (or brsoccer.__main__)\ntests/\n  test_bdd_match_queries.py\n  test_bdd_team_queries.py\n  test_bdd_player_queries.py\n  test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n  test_bdd_statistics.py\n  test_bdd_normalization.py\n  test_mcp_server.py\n```\n\nWait — the task says \"Write all code files to the current directory.\" That means files should live in the current working directory (not some nested checkout). Creating a package `brsoccer/` inside the current directory is fine.\n\nMCP tools to expose (mapping to required capabilities):\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=50)` — matches by criteria.\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)` — h2h record.\n3. `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None)` — W/D/L, goals, win rate.\n4. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, order_by_overall=True, limit=50)`.\n5. `standings(competition, season)` — computed table (Brasileirão only makes sense; also Serie B from BRF; support \"brasileirao\" sources: Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022) + historic (2003-2019)).\n6. `competition_info(competition)` — seasons covered, match counts, teams.\n7. `stats_analysis(competition=None, season=None, metric=...)` — avg goals, home win rate, biggest wins.\n8. `team_competitions(team)` — what competitions a team played in (cross-file).\n9. `last_match(team, opponent=None)` — most recent match.\n10. `find_team(name)` — resolve team name variants to canonical names (helps LLM).\n11. `derbies(season=None)` — notable derby matches (needs rival pairs table: Fla-Flu, GreNal, Clássico Majestoso, etc.)\n\nAlso a `list_datasets` / `data_summary` tool maybe. Keep focused.\n\nImportant detail on standings: Copa do Brasil and Libertadores are knockouts — standings don't apply; compute standings only for league competitions (Brasileirão Serie A/B). For \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" → standings top team. For Libertadores finals → search final stage matches.\n\n\"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\" → bottom 4 of standings.\n\nDerby detection: define a table of famous rivalries with normalized name pairs:\n- Fla-Flu: flamengo/fluminense\n- Clássico dos Milhões: flamengo/vasco\n- GreNal: gremio/internacional\n- Clássico Majestoso: corinthians/palmeiras\n- Choque-Rei: corinthians/sao paulo\n- San-São: santos/sao paulo\n- Clássico Vovô: botafogo/fluminense... (actually Botafogo vs Fluminense? No — Clássico Vovô is America-RJ vs Fluminense; Botafogo-Fluminense is \"Clássico dos Arquivos\"? hmm). Keep a small set of well-known ones to avoid errors: Fla-Flu, GreNal, Majestoso, Choque-Rei, San-São, Clássico dos Milhões (Flamengo x Vasco), Atletiba (Athletico-PR vs Coritiba), Ba-Vi (Bahia vs Vitória), Clássico-Rei? (Palmeiras vs Santos is \"Clássico da Saudade\"?). Keep: Fla-Flu, Milhões, GreNal, Majestoso, Choque-Rei, San-São, Atletiba, Ba-Vi, Come-Fogo? no. This is enough.\n\nPlayer \"club\" matching also needs normalization (e.g., FIFA Club \"Flamengo\" vs club names). FIFA club names are English-ish (\"FC Barcelona\", \"Paris Saint-Germain\") but Brazilian clubs are like \"Flamengo\"? Let me check FIFA data for Brazilian clubs.\n\nLet me also check:\n- FIFA Nationality values: \"Brazil\"\n- FIFA Club values for Brazilian clubs\n- BRF 'ht_result'/'at_result' semantics: 'LOST','WON','DRAW' — per-team half-time result. Not critical.\n\nAlso Libertadores goals are strings like \"2\" and \"-\" for missing.\n\nLet me check FIFA clubs and the BOM, plus row counts per season for standings verification. Also check historic 'Vencedor' values and the OBS column. And check whether Brasileirão 2020-2022 have all 38 rounds * 20 teams = 380 matches per season.\n\nThen design and write the code.\n\nOne more consideration: performance. Loading ~40k rows and 18k players at startup via csv module is fast (<1s). Use dataclasses, precompute indexes (team → matches, season → matches). Fine.\n\nThe MCP server: use `mcp.server.fastmcp.FastMCP` (in mcp 2.x it's `mcp.server.fastmcp.FastMCP` — let me verify import path for mcp 2.1.1). Let me check quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c656d92001l0UpuMLDq0a7Ki","sessionID":"ses_fa39bca00ffeV1iObla3wLMRAJ","messageID":"msg_05c64e0d0001EIJ3hUbHxDuwNq"}}
