).items()))\nprint(\"HIST Vencedor values:\", Counter(r['Vencedor'] for r in hist))\nprint(\"HIST 2019 home teams:\", sorted({r['Equipe_mandante'] for r in hist if r['Ano']=='2019'}))\nprint(\"BRAS 2019 home teams:\", sorted({r['home_team'] for r in bra if r['season']=='2019'}))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788256672537,"end":1788256672542}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The full client-server stdio loop works with mcp 2.x. Now I'll design and build the project.\n\n## Architecture\n\n```\nbrsoccer/                    # Python package (knowledge-graph-lite query engine)\n  __init__.py               # exports SoccerData, load_default\n  models.py                 # Match, Player dataclasses (+ enums/competitions)\n  normalize.py              # team-name normalization + TeamRegistry + DERBIES\n  dates.py                  # multi-format date parsing\n  data.py                   # CSV loaders for all 6 files -> SoccerData container\n  queries.py                # pure query engine: match/team/player/competition/stats\n  formatting.py             # human-readable answer formatting\n  mcp_server.py             # MCPServer wiring — tools that call queries + formatting\nserver.py                   # entry point: python server.py  (runs stdio)\ntests/\n  conftest.py               # fixtures: loaded SoccerData (session-scoped), MCP server+client\n  test_bdd_matches.py       # Feature: Match Queries (GWT)\n  test_bdd_teams.py         # Feature: Team Queries\n  test_bdd_players.py       # Feature: Player Queries\n  test_bdd_competitions.py  # Feature: Competition Queries\n  test_bdd_statistics.py    # Feature: Statistical Analysis\n  test_normalization_dates.py # Feature: Data normalization (names/dates)\n  test_mcp_integration.py   # Feature: MCP server protocol end-to-end (stdio)\npyproject.toml / requirements.txt\npytest.ini or pyproject config\n```\n\nWait — I should double check: \"Write all code files to the current directory.\" — everything within cwd is fine. Tests under `tests/` subdir is fine.\n\n## Key design decisions\n\n### Normalization\n```python\ndef strip_accents(s): unicodedata NFD, drop combining marks\ndef normalize_team(name):\n    1. lowercase, strip accents\n    2. remove dots (\"A.b.c.\" → \"abc\")\n    3. collapse whitespace\n    4. remove trailing state suffix: r'\\s*[-–]\\s*(AC|AL|AP|AM|BA|CE|DF|ES|GO|MA|MT|MS|MG|PA|PB|PR|PE|PI|RJ|RN|RS|RO|RR|SC|SP|SE|TO)$' (27 UFs + DF)\n    5. remove trailing club-designator words: ec, fc, ac? Hmm \"AC\" conflicts with Acre UF but after UF removal \"Aguia Negra-MS\" → \"aguia negra\". \"4 de Julho EC\" → \"4 de julho\". Removing trailing \"EC\"/\"FC\"/\"AC\"/\"FBC\"/\"SC\"? \"SC\" removed only as trailing separated token... but e.g. \"Gremio FB\"? Let me limit to a safe set: ec, fc, fbc, ac, aa, sd? I'll check what actually appears in the data. Actually let me build the registry from data and check for trailing tokens empirically.\n    6. collapse spaces/hyphens to single space\n```\nThen a canonical registry: `variant_key -> canonical_display`. All variants seen in all files get registered. Canonical display = most common \"pretty\" form (accented form preferred? or the file form?). For display, keep the original variant from the file where the variant was seen most often.\n\nAlso alias table for known equivalences:\n- \"athletico-pr\" == \"atlético-pr\" → both normalize to \"atletico pr\" vs \"athletico pr\" — differ. Map \"athletico\" → \"atletico\" for paranaense? Athletico-PR renamed from Atlético-PR. Same club. Add alias: `athletico pr` → `atletico pr`? Hmm but they're distinct spellings of the same club (renamed 2019). For h2h and stats across eras we want them unified. Yes, unify.\n- \"parana\" clube? not in data probably.\n- \"atletico mineiro\" vs \"atlético-mg\" vs \"atletico-mg\" → accent-strip handles.\n- \"cruzeiro ec\"? check data.\n- \"gremio fbpa\"? check.\n- FIFA clubs: \"Atlético Paranaense\", \"Atlético Mineiro\", \"Grêmio\", \"Atlético Goiano\"? — FIFA club matching: club search normalizes similarly (accent-insensitive, contains match).\n\nMatching for user query: \n```\ndef resolve(query) -> list[CanonicalTeam]:\n    q = normalize_team(query)\n    1. exact registry hit → [team]\n    2. alias hit\n    3. else prefix/word-boundary containment over canonical keys → candidates\n    sorted by (exact prefix first, then by match count)\n```\nAmbiguity: \"América\" matches América-MG, América-RN, América de Cali, América de México. Tools should behave deterministically: if multiple candidates, prefer Brazilian teams with more matches; or return info about ambiguity? For the benchmark \"LLM answers questions\" — best to return the best candidate plus note. I'll make `resolve_team` return ranked candidates; queries pick the top candidate but expose `find_team` tool for disambiguation, and when a tool sees ambiguity it includes \"note: other teams matched: ...\" in output. Good for the LLM.\n\nScoring for candidate ranking: exact key match > key startswith query > key contains query (word-boundary); tie-break by number of matches in dataset (more prominent team) then Brazilian UF presence.\n\n### Data model\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Match:\n    date: date | None          # parsed (None if unparseable/missing)\n    date_text: str             # original\n    time: str | None\n    competition: str           # canonical competition code: 'brasileirao', 'copa_do_brasil', 'libertadores', 'serie_b', 'serie_c', 'brasileirao_historic'? \n    competition_display: str\n    season: int | None\n    stage_or_round: str        # round number for leagues, stage for cup, round for copa\n    home: str                  # canonical team key\n    home_display: str\n    away: str\n    away_display: str\n    home_goal: int | None      # None for NA/'-'\n    away_goal: int | None\n    venue: str | None          # arena (historic only)\n    source: str                # file name\n    \n    @property played / result_for(team) -> 'W'/'D'/'L' ...\n```\n\nCompetition canonicalization: \n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv → \"Brasileirão Série A\" (2012-2022)\n- novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv → also Brasileirão Série A (2003-2019). Overlap 2012-2019 with the other file! Both cover 2012-2019. So the two files overlap. Dedupe? If we query \"Palmeiras 2015 matches\" we'd get duplicates from both files. Need dedupe strategy: key = (date, home, away) normalized → dedupe, prefer the source with more detail? The historic file has Arena; the Brasileirao file has round + time. Hmm. Dedupe on (date, home_key, away_key, competition) keeping first occurrence? That's reasonable — same match. I'll dedupe in the combined index but keep source file reference of the retained row. Actually careful: two-leg cup ties can have same teams same season but different dates — date included in key so fine. Identical date+teams in same competition is virtually always the same match recorded twice.\n\nBut wait: is the historic file \"Série A\" only? 2003-2019 Campeonato Brasileiro — yes Série A (includes 2006+ with 20 teams). OK same competition code. Dedupe makes standings correct (else double-count 2012-2019 seasons).\n\nFor standings, must ensure exactly 380 matches per season (20 teams × 38 rounds, from 2006 onward) or 462 (2003-2005, 24+ teams... actually 2003: 46 games? whatever). Dedupe prevents double counting. Let me verify counts per season after dedupe in tests: e.g., 2019 → 380 matches, champion Flamengo 90 pts (well-known fact! good BDD assertion — the spec even says Flamengo 90 pts 28W 6D 4L). That's a strong end-to-end verification.\n\nKnown facts for BDD assertions (from spec + real world):\n- 2019 Brasileirão champion: Flamengo, 90 pts (spec example says exactly: Flamengo 90 pts (28W, 6D, 4L)). \n- 2019 Santos 74 pts (22W 8D 8L) per spec. But wait — real 2019 table: Flamengo 90, Palmeiras 74, Santos 74. Spec says Santos 74 (22W 8D 8L) and Palmeiras 74 (21W 11D 6L). Hmm real: Palmeiras 74 (21W 11D 6L)? Let me not over-assert; assert Flamengo champion with 90 pts and 28W 6D 4L; assert top-3 contains Flamengo, Santos, Palmeiras.\n- 2020 relegated: Coritiba, Botafogo, Bragantino? No — 2020 relegated: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco, Sport (bottom 4). I can assert bottom 4 set = {Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco, Sport}. Let me verify with the data before hardcoding.\n\nI'll verify these against computed results before finalizing test expectations, and only assert what the data actually supports (the data is ground truth for this benchmark).\n\n- FIFA: Neymar Jr is top Brazilian (92, LW, PSG) — matches spec example. 827 Brazilian players.\n- Spec example says Alisson 89 GK Liverpool — data shows Casemiro 88... whatever; assert Neymar top with 92.\n\n### BRF file (BR-Football-Dataset.csv)\n- tournaments: Copa do Brasil, Serie A, Serie B, Serie C. It overlaps with Brasileirao_Matches (Serie A) and Brazilian_Cup_Matches (Copa do Brasil). It adds corner/attack/shot stats. What seasons? 2023-09-24 dates... let me check range. Need to check. If it's 2023+ Serie A, then no overlap with the 2012-2022 Brasileirão file — complementary. But its Copa do Brasil rows might overlap with Brazilian_Cup_Matches (2012-2023?). Need to check seasons of both.\n\nCompetition codes:\n- serie_a: Brasileirao_Matches + novo_campeonato (deduped) [+ BRF \"Serie A\" if non-overlapping... but BRF \"Serie A\" might be 2023 season — complementary; include with dedupe anyway]\n- copa_do_brasil: Brazilian_Cup_Matches + BRF \"Copa do Brasil\" (dedupe)\n- libertadores: Libertadores_Matches\n- serie_b, serie_c: BRF only\n\nDedupe within competition across sources via (date, home_key, away_key). BRF has corner stats — keep the row with more data? If duplicates, prefer BRF (has extra stats) or keep both? For match listing, dedupe; stats like corners are per-match. I'll prefer keeping one row — priority: BRF (richer) > newer files? Simplest: iterate sources in priority order and skip already-seen keys. But careful: dedupe across \"serie a\" from BRF vs Brasileirão file — check overlap after load; test data counts.\n\nHmm, actually careful with Copa do Brasil: two-legged ties — two matches with same teams but different dates — fine, key includes date. But what about a final with same date, two legs at same stadium on same date? No.\n\nEdge: penalty shootouts not in data. Fine.\n\n### Half-time results in BRF: ht_result/at_result are per-team (WON/LOST/DRAW). Skip; not required.\n\n### queries.py functions\n```\nclass SoccerData:\n    matches: list[Match]          # deduped\n    players: list[Player]\n    registry: TeamRegistry\n    by_team: dict[team_key, list[Match]]\n    by_season: ...\n    \n    # API\n    find_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=None) -> list[Match]\n    head_to_head(a, b, competition=None, season=None) -> H2H summary + matches\n    team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue='all') -> TeamStats (matches, w/d/l, gf, ga, win_rate, home/away splits)\n    team_competitions(team) -> dict[comp, count, seasons]\n    last_match(team, opponent=None) -> Match|None\n    standings(competition='serie_a', season=...) -> list[TableRow] (points, w/d/l, gf, ga, gd, played)\n    players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall, limit, sort)\n    brazilian_club_overview() -> Brazilian players at Brazilian clubs aggregated\n    competition_summary(competition) -> seasons, teams count, matches count\n    avg_goals(competition=None, season=None) -> float + home win rate\n    biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit) -> list\n    best_records(venue='home'|'away', competition=None, min matches) -> ranked teams by win rate\n    derbies(season=None, competition=None) -> list of (derby_name, matches)\n    relegation(competition, season, n=4)\n```\n\n### Derby table (normalized canonical keys):\n```\nDERBIES = [\n (\"Fla-Flu\", (\"flamengo\", \"fluminense\")),\n (\"Clássico dos Milhões\", (\"flamengo\", \"vasco\")),\n (\"Clássico Majestoso\", (\"corinthians\", \"palmeiras\")),\n (\"Choque-Rei\", (\"corinthians\", \"sao paulo\")),\n (\"San-São\", (\"santos\", \"sao paulo\")),\n (\"GreNal\", (\"gremio\", \"internacional\")),\n (\"Atletiba\", (\"athletico pr\"/\"atletico pr\", \"coritiba\")),\n (\"Ba-Vi\", (\"bahia\", \"vitoria\")),\n (\"Clássico dos Gigantes\"? maybe not)\n (\"Come-Fogo\"? no)\n (\"Clássico Alvinegro\"? no)\n]\n```\nKeep it small and correct. Also \"Derby Paulista\" generic? No — specific pairs above suffice.\n\n### FIFA player club matching for Brazilian clubs\nFIFA club names: \"Flamengo\"? Let me check — BR-ish clubs printed: Grêmio 20, Atlético Mineiro 20, Fluminense 20, Santos 20, Internacional 20, Botafogo 20, Bahia 20, Atlético Paranaense 20... and \"Flamengo\" didn't appear in my grep list? My filter had 'Flamengo' — it's not in output... wait the output shows only 10 items ([:25] should show more). Sorted by count desc, ties — 'Grêmio' 20 etc. Flamengo should have 20-26 players. Hmm the sort with -x[1] then insertion order... it printed 10 entries? I limited [:25] but only 10 shown because... the dict comprehension matched few clubs? 'Flamengo' isn't in there! Odd. Let me check directly. Actually maybe FIFA club is \"Flamengo\" — should match. Let me search the data for what clubs Brazilians at Rio clubs have. I'll check when building. Also \"São Paulo\" club in FIFA might be \"São Paulo\" — my filter 'São Paulo' should match... not shown. Only 10 entries shown though list sorted [:25]. Strange — maybe there were exactly 10 matched. But 'Flamengo' and 'São Paulo' missing. Perhaps FIFA dataset uses different names? Let me check directly in the build phase.\n\nPlayer club filter: normalize both sides (accent-strip, lowercase) and match by containment (club name contains query or equals). For \"Brazilian clubs\" concept: maintain a set of club names from the match data (Brazilian teams), and map FIFA club strings to them via normalized equality/containment. E.g., FIFA \"Atlético Paranaense\" → registry \"atletico pr\"? Normalized \"atletico paranaense\" vs \"atletico pr\" — not equal. Need alias: \"atletico paranaense\" → \"athletico pr\"/\"atletico pr\". Add a few manual club aliases: \n- \"atletico paranaense\" → \"atletico pr\"\n- \"atletico mineiro\" → \"atletico mg\"\n- \"atletico goiano\"? → \"atletico go\"\n- \"sao paulo\" → \"sao paulo\" ✓\n- \"flamengo\" ✓\n- \"gremio\" ✓ (FIFA \"Grêmio\" → \"gremio\" ✓)\n- \"palmeiras\" ✓\n- \"corinthians\" ✓\n- \"santos\" ✓ (FIFA \"Santos\" — also Mexican \"Santos Laguna\" — containment must be word-boundary/exact to avoid matching Santos Laguna! Use exact normalized equality first; containment fallback with word boundary: \"santos\" as full token in \"santos laguna\" would match... For club filter use exact-match, plus registered-team alias match. \"Santos\" exact → \"santos\". Good.)\n\n### Dates\nparse_date handles: \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\", \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\", ISO \"2012-05-19T...\" maybe. Return date or None. Also parse \"season\" int with fallback.\n\n### MCP tools (11-13 tools)\n1. find_team(name) — resolve variants → canonical names + match counts (disambiguation helper)\n2. search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit) — formatted list\n3. head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition, season)\n4. team_stats(team, season, competition, venue: all|home|away)\n5. last_match(team, opponent)\n6. team_competitions(team)\n7. standings(competition, season, top_n)  (also returns relegation zone)\n8. relegation(competition, season) — or fold into standings? Keep separate tool `relegation`.\n9. search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, limit)\n10. club_overview() — Brazilian players grouped by Brazilian club (spec example wants this)\n11. competition_info(competition)\n12. competition_stats(competition, season) — avg goals, home win rate\n13. biggest_wins(competition, season, limit)\n14. best_records(venue, competition, season, min_matches)\n15. derbies(season, competition)\n16. data_summary() — datasets loaded, row counts, coverage (data coverage success criterion)\n\nThat's 16 tools — good coverage of all 5 categories + data summary. All return formatted strings (text content) since \"Returns properly formatted responses\" is a requirement. Maybe return both text and structured? FastMCP v2 auto-creates structured output from return type. Return strings (text). For tests, call underlying query layer for structure assertions + a couple of MCP-level text assertions.\n\n### Performance\nLoad at module import in server (lazy singleton). CSV parse of ~40k rows < 1s. Indexes precomputed. Fine.\n\n### Testing (BDD GWT)\npytest with Gherkin-style structure using docstrings and Given/When/Then comments — the classic pattern:\n\n```python\n# Feature: Match Queries\n# Scenario: Find matches between two teams\n\nclass TestFindMatchesBetweenTwoTeams:\n    def test_when_searching_flamengo_vs_fluminense(self, soccer_data):\n        # Given the match data is loaded\n        # When I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Fluminense\"\n        matches = soccer_data.find_matches(team=\"Flamengo\", opponent=\"Fluminense\")\n        # Then I should receive a list of matches\n        assert matches\n        # And each match should have date, scores, and competition\n        for m in matches:\n            assert m.date is not None\n            assert m.competition\n```\n\nI'll structure tests as class-per-scenario with explicit Given/When/Then comments and asserts, mirroring the gherkin in the spec. Include:\n\n- test_bdd_matches.py: \n  - Scenario: find matches between two teams (Fla-Flu)\n  - Scenario: matches by team+season (Palmeiras 2023? wait — Brasileirão file covers 2012-2022 only; 2023 Palmeiras matches would come from BRF Serie A if it covers 2023. Need to check BRF seasons first! If BRF is 2023 only, then Palmeiras 2023 exists. Verify.)\n  - Scenario: date-range queries\n  - Scenario: by competition (copa do brasil finals = round \"final\"? Check copa rounds — samples show rounds 1-8. Hmm \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" — round values seen: '1'-'8'. Wait is there a \"final\" round? Let me check unique rounds fully. The printed sorted list showed only 1..8 (key=str). But there might be non-numeric rounds like \"final\". It printed: ['1','2','3','4','5','6','7','8'] — that's it? With key=str, 'final' would sort after digits... the list shows only 8 elements, so rounds are purely numeric 1-8? Hmm but printed list was `sorted(..., key=str)[:20]` — 8 elements only. So no \"final\" string. Copa do Brasil in this dataset uses numbered rounds 1-8 (round 8 might not exist... 2012-2023 era: rounds 1-7 + final=round 8?). Hmm — need to check max round and per-season. Whatever — expose stage/round filter; LLM can figure \"finals\" via round number. Maybe I should check what round the final is (in modern Copa do Brasil: rounds are numbered; final is round 8? Actually 2023 Copa do Brasil had rounds 1-7 where 7=fourth round... no. The Copa do Brasil format: 7 rounds total with final being... let me check the data: count matches per round per season. I'll check in build phase and encode knowledge: \"final\" round number mapping. Simplest: stage filter matches substring on round text; and I can add logic: competition_info(copa) reports round counts. And maybe add a convenience: search_matches(stage=\"final\") → matches copa rounds where round text contains \"final\"? If rounds are numeric, \"final\" won't match. I'll check data first.)\n\n- test_bdd_teams.py: team stats (Corinthians home 2022 — spec example: 19 matches, 11W 5D 3L, 28 GF, 15 GA, 57.9% — verify against data before asserting; assert computed values match spec example), h2h Palmeiras vs Santos, goals-most team in Serie A 2023 (if BRF has 2023).\n- test_bdd_players.py: search by name \"Gabriel Barbosa\" (FIFA has \"Gabriel Barbosa\"? earlier grep showed Gabriel Jesus, Gabriel Paulista, Gabriel, Gabriel Boschilia, Gabriel Silva — no \"Gabriel Barbosa\"! The spec says \"Who is Gabriel Barbosa?\" → search by name → result: not found but similar \"Gabriel\" players found. Test should assert fuzzy search returns Gabriel* candidates. Also \"Neymar\" search. Brazilian players count 827. Top Brazilian = Neymar 92. Brazilians at Flamengo (check FIFA club name for Flamengo!).\n- test_bdd_competitions.py: standings 2019 (Flamengo 90 pts champion — verify), relegation 2020 (bottom 4 — verify from data), competition info seasons coverage (2003-2023 overall), cross-file (team played in Libertadores + Brasileirão + Copa).\n- test_bdd_statistics.py: avg goals brasileirão (compute, sanity range 2.0-3.0), home win rate range 40-60%, biggest wins (assert sorted by margin desc; verify top), best home/away records, derbies 2023 (Fla-Flu in 2023 — Brasileirão file ends 2022! Fla-Flu 2023 would be in BRF if 2023. Spec example: \"2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense\" — that's probably from BRF Serie A 2023. Verify.)\n- test_normalization_dates.py: variants (\"Palmeiras-SP\" == \"palmeiras\" == \"PALMEIRAS\"), accents (\"Grêmio\" == \"Gremio\"), spaced dash (\"América - MG\"), dotted (\"A.b.c. - RN\" == \"ABC - RN\"), dates 3 formats, NA goals handling, UTF-8 names preserved in display.\n- test_mcp_integration.py: real stdio client session — initialize, list tools (≥16?), call search_matches & search_players & standings, assert text content. Plus performance guard: call tool latency < 2s simple, < 5s aggregate.\n\n### Performance test\nMeasure: simple lookup < 2s (should be ~ms), standings/aggregate < 5s. Use time.monotonic in test. But the MCP integration test spawns a subprocess (server load ~1s) — measure call latency after initialize, excluding startup. Fine.\n\n### Standings correctness verification\nBefore writing tests, compute standings 2019 from my (planned) dedupe logic and compare with known truth: Flamengo 90 pts (28W 6D 4L, 90 pts? 28*3+6=90 ✓). Let me make sure the two overlapping files dedupe correctly for 2019. 2019: novo file has 380 matches; Brasileirao file 2012-2022 has 2019: 380. After dedupe → 380. \n\nBut note: novo file team names for 2019 use \"Athletico-PR\"? and Brasileirao file uses \"Atletico-PR\" (unaccented)? If dedupe key uses canonical key with alias mapping (athletico pr → atletico pr), dedupe works. If not, we'd get 760 matches for 2019 and wrong standings. CRITICAL: alias mapping must be applied before dedupe. Let me check team names in both files for 2019.\n\nAlso check: does novo file have \"Gols_mandante\" numeric always? yes (no non-int found).\n\nVencedor column: \"Mandante/Visitante/Empate\" (+ maybe \"-\" for missing?). Check values.\n\nLet me now do a thorough data exploration pass to pin all these facts, then write the code.\n\nAlso check BRF seasons and whether \"Serie A\" 2023 exists (for Palmeiras 2023 & Fla-Flu 2023 questions), and FIFA club exact names for Brazilian clubs (Flamengo present?).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c67476b001NUZU3meMkWAh2a","sessionID":"ses_fa39bca00ffeV1iObla3wLMRAJ","messageID":"msg_05c65f9f40014RnsXCJESmNYdD"}}
