in clubs.most_common(40):\n    print(f\"  {c!r}: {n}\")\nallc = Counter(row['Club'] for row in rows)\nprint(\"\\n'Flamengo' anywhere:\", [c for c in allc if 'lamengo' in c])\nprint(\"'São Paulo'/'Sao Paulo' anywhere:\", [c for c in allc if 'ao Paulo' in c])\nprint(\"'Palmeiras' anywhere:\", [c for c in allc if 'almeiras' in c])\nprint(\"'Corinthians':\", [c for c in allc if 'orinthians' in c])\nprint(\"'Vasco':\", [c for c in allc if 'asco' in c])\nprint(\"'Gremio':\", [c for c in allc if 'remio' in c])\nprint(\"'Ceara':\", [c for c in allc if 'eara' in c])\nprint(\"'Fortaleza':\", [c for c in allc if 'ortaleza' in c])\nprint(\"'Goiás':\", [c for c in allc if 'oi' in c and 's' in c][:10])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788256852713,"end":1788256852728}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a complete picture. Design decisions:\n\n**Team canonicalization pipeline** (order matters):\n\n1. Decode: `deacc` (strip accents), lowercase, strip, remove periods (`A.b.c.` → `abc`), collapse whitespace. Also strip trailing parenthetical stuff like \"(antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\"? Copa has \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\". Base becomes \"boavista sport club antigo esporte clube barreira\" — ugly but harmless; it dedupes consistently. Could strip parenthetical: remove `(...)` content. Yes — remove parenthesized segments.\n\n2. Remove common club-designator words — but ONLY as whole trailing tokens from a safe list: `ec`, `fc`, `ac`, `sc`?, `aa`, `fbc`... Danger: \"ac\" removal would turn \"botafogo ac\"? Careful with \"AC\" = Acre. But as a *trailing token after removing UF* — e.g. \"EC Bahia\" → base \"ec bahia\" → remove leading/trailing \"ec\" → \"bahia\". \"Fortaleza EC\" → \"fortaleza\". \"Santa Cruz FC\" → \"santa cruz\". \"Vitoria EC\" → \"vitoria\" — hmm but \"Vitoria EC\" (BRF) vs \"Vitoria\" (HIST, BA club) — same ✓. \"Vitoria da Conquista\" keep (distinct club, different base ✓). \"Vitoria ES\"?? — \"Vitoria - ES\"? that's Rio Branco-ES? No wait — \"Vitoria ES\" might be \"Vitória-ES\" a small Espírito Santo club. Distinct from Vitória-BA ✓ (base+uf distinct).\n   Designator set: `ec`, `fc`, `ac`?? Hmm \"Macae Esporte FC\" → \"macae esporte\". \"Guarani FC\"? Not seen. I'll include: ec, fc, ac, aa, sd? Keep to {ec, fc, ac, ca?} — \"CA\" (Clube Atlético) e.g. \"CA Paranaense\"? not seen. Use {ec, fc, ac} but only strip if the remaining base is non-empty AND the token count > 1 after removal. Note \"AC\" as trailing token: \"Botafogo AC\" would be Botafogo-AC (small club)... not in data. Risk acceptable; I'll verify no collisions result by testing the full registry build: assert canonical key sets don't merge known-distinct clubs. Better: after building, eyeball the big-club keys.\n\n   Actually safer: strip designators {ec, fc} only (both seen), plus handle full-name aliases via explicit table. \"ac\" not seen in data; skip it.\n\n3. Extract UF suffix: pattern `(base) [-–] (uf)` where uf ∈ 27 UFs → (base, uf).\n\n4. Apply **full-name alias table** (normalized, post-step-3 base without UF):\n   - `atletico mineiro` → key `atletico mg`\n   - `atletico goianiense` → `atletico go`\n   - `atletico paranaense` → `atletico pr`; `athletico paranaense` → `atletico pr`\n   - `sport recife` → `sport` (BRAS \"Sport-PE\" → base \"sport\")\n   - `vasco da gama` → `vasco`\n   - `nautico capibaribe` → `nautico pe` (BRF; Nautico-PE is the big one; \"Nautico RR\" stays distinct)\n   - `fluminense de feira`?? That's a different club (Fluminense de Feira-BA) — keep distinct as `fluminense de feira` — base is unique, fine, no alias.\n   - `flamengo do piaui` → `flamengo pi` (Copa \"Flamengo - PI\" and \"Flamengo do Piauí - PI\" same club)\n   - `portuguesa desportos` → `portuguesa`\n   - `gremio esportivo sapucaiense` — distinct small club, keep.\n   - `santa cruz fc` → base \"santa cruz\" + uf? \"Santa Cruz FC\" (BRF, no UF) — bare \"santa cruz\" — ambiguous base (PE, RN, RS) — bare resolution needed (→ PE, the big one). \"Santa Cruz RN\"/\"Santa Cruz RS\" have UF → distinct.\n   - `fortaleza ec` / `fortaleza fc` → `fortaleza` (both are Fortaleza-CE; \"Fortaleza FC\" might be a different club?? There's \"Fortaleza FC\" in... hmm, Argentina? No — in BRF both \"Fortaleza EC\" and \"Fortaleza FC\" exist. Probably both = Fortaleza-CE. Check their co-occurrence per season/match. Minor risk, acceptable: both map to `fortaleza`.)\n   - `ec internacional sc`? That's Internacional-SC (distinct from Inter-RS) — base \"internacional sc\"?? wait \"EC Internacional SC\" → strip \"ec\" → \"internacional sc\" → hmm the \"SC\" here is Santa Catarina suffix! Pattern: trailing 2-letter code \"sc\" ∈ UF → (base \"internacional\", uf SC) → key `internacional sc` ✓ distinct from `internacional rs`. But bare \"Internacional\" (LIB, BRAS \"Internacional-RS\" → base \"internacional\") → bare resolution → dominant RS ✓.\n   - `atletico cearense` / `fc atletico cearense` → `atletico cearense` — distinct club from \"Atletico - CE\"? Copa has \"Atlético Cearense - CE\" — same club → both → base `atletico cearense` + uf ce → `atletico cearense ce`?? Hmm. \"Atlético Cearense - CE\" → (base \"atletico cearense\", uf CE). \"FC Atlético Cearense\" (BRF, no UF) → strip fc → \"atletico cearense\" — no UF. Base+noUF. If base \"atletico cearense\" is unambiguous (only one uf variant seen) → key = \"atletico cearense\" for both ✓.\n   - `botafogo rj` vs bare `botafogo` → bare → dominant RJ ✓; `botafogo pb`, `botafogo sp` distinct.\n   - `bragantino` (BRF bare) vs `Bragantino - PA` vs `Red Bull Bragantino`?? BRF has \"Bragantino\" and \"Bragantino PA\". Modern RB Bragantino = Bragantino-SP. HIST has \"Bragantino\"? 2020 relegated per spec — Bragantino SP played Série A 2020+. HIST ends 2019 (Bragantino-SP in Série B until 2019). Copa \"Bragantino - PA\" is Clube Atlético Bragantino-PA (small). BRF bare \"Bragantino\" = the SP one (Série A 2020-2023). Bare resolution → count-dominant SP ✓ (since Bragantino-PA appears rarely).\n   - `america mg` / `america rn` (BRF spelled with space+UF) → pattern: trailing \"mg\" — is \"mg\" a UF token? \"America MG\" → last token \"mg\" ∈ UF → (america, MG) → key `america mg` ✓. Copa \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\" → base \"atletico mineiro\" → alias → `atletico mg` ✓.\n\n5. **Ambiguous-base bare resolution**: bases observed with ≥2 UFs → those bases REQUIRE uf in key. A bare occurrence of such a base maps to the count-dominant UF variant (deterministic; ties → more recent appearances? no—alphabetical). Exception: 'athletico' (bare) → 'atletico pr' ALWAYS (rename spelling is unambiguous), applied via alias BEFORE ambiguity resolution. Similarly bare 'atletico' in LIB → count-dominant = MG... but wait, is bare \"Atlético\" in LIB actually Atlético-MG? LIB list shows 'Atlético-MG' (with suffix) AND 'Athletico' (bare). I didn't see bare 'Atlético' in LIB list — only 'Athletico' bare. ✓ So alias 'athletico'→'atletico pr' handles all LIB bare cases correctly. For bare 'atletico' — appears where? The 2013 mystery: BRAS 2013 \"atletico\" bare keys from earlier debug — wait those were POST-strip keys from MY prototype (I stripped UF). Raw BRAS always has suffix. HIST: \"Atlético-MG\" always suffixed; no bare atletico except... HIST atletico list shows ['Atlético-GO', 'Atlético-MG'] — suffixed. And \"Athletico-PR\" (2019). So bare 'atletico'/'athletico' raw only in LIB ('Athletico') and maybe BRF ('Atletico Acreano' is a different club — full name). Good.\n\n   Count-dominance for ambiguous bases — compute counts from raw occurrences (with UF) per variant:\n   - santos: SP >> AP ✓\n   - flamengo: RJ >> PI ✓\n   - botafogo: RJ >> PB, SP ✓\n   - internacional: RS >> SC ✓\n   - america: MG vs RN — América-MG historically bigger (Série A 2012-2016...). Let me compute actual counts. If RN wins by count, bare \"América\" would map to RN — wrong-ish, but bare \"América\" barely occurs (Copa uses \"América - RN\" suffixed;