ing.text","text":"FIFA data (FIFA 19 era) has these Brazilian clubs: Grêmio, Atlético Mineiro, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Internacional, América FC (Minas Gerais), Botafogo, Bahia, Paraná, Atlético Paranaense, Vitória, Sport Club do Recife, Chapecoense, Ceará Sporting Club. Flamengo, Palmeiras, Corinthians, São Paulo, Vasco are NOT in this FIFA dataset (license issues in FIFA 19 — those clubs were licensed in PES). Important to KNOW: \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" → honest answer: no Flamengo players in this FIFA snapshot; the tool should say that and suggest what IS available.\n\nFIFA club aliases needed to map to match-data team keys:\n- \"Grêmio\" → gremio ✓ (plain)\n- \"Atlético Mineiro\" → atletico mg\n- \"Atlético Paranaense\" → atletico pr\n- \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → america mg (parenthetical strip → \"américa fc\" hmm! \"(Minas Gerais)\" removed leaves \"América FC\" → base \"america fc\" → strip fc → \"america\" — AMBIGUOUS base! Need special alias: \"america fc (minas gerais)\"... I must apply the parenthetical CONTENT aware alias, or map the whole raw string. I'll alias on full normalized string BEFORE parenthetical strip: \"america fc minas gerais\" → \"america mg\". Order: normalize (keep parenthetical content inline), check FULL alias table first, THEN structural transforms. Wait but \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" — full string alias would need entries for every parenthetical. Better: FIRST strip parentheticals for structural transform, but ALSO check a pre-strip alias table for special FIFA strings. I'll put \"america fc minas gerais\" (pre-strip, dots removed, spaces collapsed) in CLUB_ALIASES applied before parenthetical removal.\n- \"Sport Club do Recife\" → sport (alias)\n- \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → ceara (strip \"sporting club\"? alias: \"ceara sporting club\" → \"ceara\")\n- \"Santos\" → santos — but FIFA also has \"Santos Laguna\" (Mexican)! Player-club search for \"Santos\" must NOT match \"Santos Laguna\" — exact-equality matching after aliases: \"santos laguna\" ≠ \"santos\" ✓. But bare \"santos\" in FIFA = Brazilian Santos ✓ (Mexican club is always \"Santos Laguna\" full).\n- \"Botafogo\" FIFA = Botafogo-RJ? FIFA 19 has Botafogo (RJ). ✓ bare → dominant RJ.\n- \"Bahia\" → bahia ✓\n- \"Paraná\" → parana ✓ (Paraná Clube)\n- \"Vitória\" → vitoria ✓ (BA)\n- \"Chapecoense\" ✓\n- \"Cruzeiro\" ✓, \"Fluminense\" ✓, \"Internacional\" ✓ (FIFA also has \"Internacional de Madrid\"? maybe — check \"Inter\" clubs... \"Internacional\" exact = Brazilian one. There's 'SC Internacional' (Argentine?) hmm. Exact match handles.)\n\nSo: player club matching = normalize FIFA club string through the SAME canonicalizer + club aliases → team key → if it maps to a registry team key, we can join players↔matches (cross-file query requirement!). \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → \"america mg\" joins with match data ✓. Nice: this enables \"cross-file queries (player + match data)\" success criterion, e.g., team_stats for \"Atlético Mineiro\" + its FIFA players.\n\nFor player search by club where club is NOT a Brazilian registry team (e.g. \"Real Madrid\"), just filter FIFA Club string by exact-normalized or containment match. Club parameter semantics: match against FIFA club names (exact normalized equality OR containment when unambiguous?). I'll do: exact normalized equality first; if no hits, prefix/word-boundary containment. Alias map Brazilian names too so query \"Atlético MG\" finds FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" players: canonicalize the QUERY the same way → \"atletico mg\" → then match FIFA clubs whose canonical form == \"atletico mg\" (via alias \"atletico mineiro\"). So the join is through the canonicalizer on both sides. For non-Brazilian clubs (no alias), query \"Real Madrid\" → \"real madrid\" == FIFA \"Real Madrid\" ✓.\n\n**Now — write the code.** Full layout:\n\n```\nbrsoccer/\n  __init__.py\n  dates.py\n  normalize.py\n  models.py\n  data.py\n  queries.py\n  formatting.py\n  mcp_server.py\nserver.py\ntests/...\npyproject.toml\nrequirements.txt\n```\n\n### models.py\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Match:\n    date: date | None\n    date_text: str\n    kickoff: str | None\n    competition: str          # code\n    competition_display: str\n    season: int | None\n    stage: str                # round / stage text\n    home: str                 # canonical key\n    away: str\n    home_display: str\n    away_display: str\n    home_goal: int | None\n    away_goal: int | None\n    venue: str | None\n    source: str\n\n    def involves(self, team_key) -> bool\n    def result_for(self, team_key) -> 'W'|'D'|'L'|None\n    @property margin\n    @property is_played\n    @property score_text\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Player:\n    fifa_id: int\n    name: str\n    age: int|None\n    nationality: str\n    overall: int\n    potential: int\n    club: str               # raw FIFA club\n    club_key: str|None      # canonical if maps to registry/Brazilian alias\n    position: str\n    jersey: int|None\n    height: str / weight: str (raw strings like \"5'7\", \"159lbs\" — keep raw)\n    value: str, wage: str (raw €)\n\n@dataclass\nclass TableRow: team, display, played, win, draw, loss, gf, ga, gd, points, position, source_note\n```\n\n### normalize.py\n```python\nUF_CODES = frozenset(...)  # 27\nCLUB_DESIGNATORS = {\"ec\", \"fc\"}  # trailing tokens to strip (extend cautiously)\n\n# applied to the FULL normalized string (before parenthetical strip):\nFULL_ALIASES = {\n    \"america fc minas gerais\": (\"america\", \"MG\"),   # FIFA\n    \"boavista sport club antigo esporte clube barreira\": (\"boavista sport club\", None)?? — parenthetical strip handles: remove \"(...)\" → \"boavista sport club - rj\" → base \"boavista sport club\", uf RJ. fine, no alias needed.\n}\n\n# applied to base after UF extraction:\nBASE_ALIASES = {\n    \"atletico mineiro\": \"atletico mg\",   # wait — base already has no uf; alias target key form\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, cleaner: a single function `canonical_key(raw) -> (key, uf_used: bool)`:\n\n1. `s = preprocess(raw)`: strip accents? — NO, keep accents for display, but for key: deacc, lower, remove dots/quotes, collapse spaces, strip parenthesized groups (record them separately for FULL alias check BEFORE removal).\n2. Check `SPECIAL_ALIASES[full_string_including_parenthetical]` → return key.\n3. Extract trailing UF.\n4. Strip designator tokens from base edges (ec, fc).\n5. Check `BASE_ALIASES[base]` (e.g., \"atletico mineiro\"→\"atletico mg\"; \"vasco da gama\"→\"vasco\"; \"sport recife\"→\"sport\"; \"athletico paranaense\"→\"atletico pr\"; \"atletico paranaense\"→\"atletico pr\"; \"atletico goianiense\"→\"atletico go\"; \"flamengo do piaui\"→\"flamengo pi\"; \"nautico capibaribe\"→\"nautico pe\"; \"portuguesa desportos\"→\"portuguesa\"; \"ceara sporting club\"→\"ceara\"; \"sport club do recife\"→\"sport\"; \"sport club corinthians paulista\"→\"corinthians\"; \"clube de regatas do flamengo\"→\"flamengo\"; \"botafogo de futebol e regatas\"→\"botafogo rj\"?? careful: bare botafogo resolves by dominance → rj. Set → \"botafogo\".)\n   - \"athletico\" → \"atletico pr\" (bare rename form; also suffixed \"athletico pr\" natural via uf+base... base \"athletico\" + uf PR → key candidate \"athletico pr\" — but canonical key should be \"atletico pr\" (with the atlético spelling since most data uses it). So normalize \"athletico\"→\"atletico\" in base BEFORE uf attach, EXCEPT bare. Hmm: \"Athletico-PR\" → base \"athletico\" uf PR → base alias maps \"athletico\"→\"atletico\" → key \"atletico pr\" ✓. Bare \"Athletico\" (LIB) → base alias \"athletico\"→\"atletico\" → bare \"atletico\" → ambiguous-base dominance → \"atletico mg\" ✗✗ WRONG (should be PR!).\n   Fix: two aliases: base-with-uf form (\"athletico pr\" → \"atletico pr\" — automatic if base→\"atletico\"), and BARE \"athletico\" → \"atletico pr\" special (because the bare form only exists post-rename for PR). So: BASE_ALIASES maps \"athletico\"→\"atletico\" (spelling unification), and BARE_ALIASES maps \"athletico\"→\"atletico pr\" applied only when no UF. Order: if uf: base→unify spelling→key f\"{base} {uf}\". if no uf: check BARE_ALIASES first (\"athletico\"→\"atletico pr\"), else ambiguous-dominance, else key=base.\n   Wait, but what about COPA \"Athletico Paranaense - PR\" → base \"athletico paranaense\" uf PR → alias \"athletico paranaense\"→\"atletico\"?? then key \"atletico pr\" ✓. Set BASE_ALIASES[\"athletico paranaense\"]=\"atletico\" and [\"atletico paranaense\"]=\"atletico\", [\"atletico mineiro\"]=\"atletico\", [\"atletico goianiense\"]=\"atletico\". Then uf attaches: \"atletico mg/pr/go\" ✓✓✓ elegant! And bare \"Atletico Mineiro\" (BRF, no uf) → base \"atletico mineiro\" → alias → \"atletico\" — bare ambiguous! → dominance → maybe MG by count ✓ (BRF \"Atletico Mineiro\" IS MG). But bare \"atletico paranaense\"→\"atletico\"→dominance→MG ✗ (should be PR)! So aliases must produce the FULL key directly: BASE_ALIASES[\"atletico mineiro\"]=\"atletico mg\", [\"atletico paranaense\"]=\"atletico pr\", [\"athletico paranaense\"]=\"atletico pr\", [\"atletico goianiense\"]=\"atletico go\", [\"atletico cearense\"]=\"atletico cearense\" (distinct small club; actually is it Atlético-CE? There IS an \"Atlético Cearense\"... but COPA \"Atlético Cearense - CE\" separate club from \"Atletico - CE\"? Hmm \"Atlético - CE\"... is that a thing? From ambiguity list: atletico UFs {AC,BA,ES,GO,MG,PR} — CE not included, so \"Atlético Cearense - CE\" base is \"atletico cearense\" and plain \"Atletico - CE\" doesn't exist. Fine.) — and when a uf IS attached to these full-name forms (e.g., \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\" from COPA), extract uf first → base \"atletico mineiro\" → alias → \"atletico mg\"; then the extracted uf \"MG\" already consumed in alias target — attach only if target doesn't end with uf: final key = alias_target if alias endswith uf else f\"{alias} {uf}\".\n   \n   Simplify: key = resolve(base, uf):\n   ```\n   if base in BASE_ALIASES: target = BASE_ALIASES[base]  # e.g. \"atletico mg\"\n       return target if uf is None or target endswith(\" \"+uf-ish) else target  # aliases are complete keys; ignore extracted uf (it's redundant)\n   if base == \"athletico\": base = \"atletico\"  # spelling unification for suffixed forms\n   if uf: return f\"{base} {uf}\"  ... but ONLY if base is \"ambiguous\"? Or always keep uf when present?\n   ```\n   Wait — if I ALWAYS keep uf when present: \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras sp\" vs HIST \"Palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\" — MISMATCH. So: keep uf only when base is ambiguous (in the global ambiguity set built from data), else drop it. The ambiguity set is data-driven: bases seen with ≥2 distinct UFs OR (base seen bare AND with a uf AND base is \"collision-prone\")... hmm no: \"palmeiras\" appears bare (HIST) and with SP (BRAS). Is bare \"Palmeiras\" = Palmeiras-SP? Yes obviously. Rule: base ambiguous ⇔ seen with ≥2 DISTINCT UFs. Palmeiras: only SP → not ambiguous → uf dropped → \"palmeiras\" ✓. Santos: SP + AP → ambiguous → suffixed keys \"santos sp\"/\"santos ap\"; bare \"Santos\" (LIB/HIST) → dominance → \"santos sp\" ✓✓. Atletico: 6 UFs → ambiguous; \"Atletico-MG\" → \"atletico mg\" ✓; bare doesn't occur except via athletico-alias → PR ✓.\n\n   But HIST \"Atlético-MG\" → \"atletico mg\" ✓, BRAS \"Atletico-MG\" → \"atletico mg\" ✓ — match! COPA \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\" → alias → \"atletico mg\" ✓. BRF \"Atletico Mineiro\" → alias → \"atletico mg\" ✓. LIB \"Atlético-MG\" ✓. FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" ✓. All unify.\n\n   Ambiguity set must ALSO include alias targets' bases — e.g. is base \"atletico\" ambiguous? yes (6 ufs). Base \"atletico mineiro\" — seen only bare → its alias target includes uf explicitly, no ambiguity.\n\n   Dominance for bare ambiguous bases: count occurrences per (base,uf) across all match rows (FIFA clubs too?). Use match rows only. Deterministic.\n\n   Edge: BRF \"Coritiba PR\" → uf PR kept → \"coritiba pr\"; but base \"coritiba\" elsewhere is bare/unique (\"Coritiba\" HIST/BRAS \"Coritiba-PR\"→ base coritiba uf PR — base coritiba seen with only PR → NOT ambiguous → drop uf → \"coritiba\" ✓ and BRF \"Coritiba PR\" → also \"coritiba\" ✓ consistent because ambiguity is GLOBAL: coritiba has only PR → not ambiguous → drop. ✓✓ The global ambiguity rule keeps everything consistent.)\n\n   BRF \"Fluminense RJ\" vs \"Fluminense\" vs \"Fluminense PI\" vs \"Fluminense De Feira\": base \"fluminense\" seen with UFs {RJ, PI} → ambiguous → \"fluminense rj\"/\"fluminense pi\"; bare \"Fluminense\" → dominance RJ ✓ (LIB/HIST/BRAS bare Flamengo... Fluminense 1108 bare) ✓. \"Fluminense De Feira\" → base \"fluminense de feira\" unique → own key ✓.\n\n   BRF \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → base \"vasco da gama\" uf RJ → alias \"vasco da gama\"→\"vasco\"... alias target \"vasco\" is a COMPLETE key; extracted uf RJ redundant → final \"vasco\" ✓ (HIST \"Vasco\" ✓, BRAS \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" ✓, LIB \"Vasco\" ✓, COPA \"Vasco da Gama - RJ\" ✓). Is base \"vasco\" ambiguous? only RJ → fine.\n\n   BRF \"Internacional RS\" → base internacional uf RS → ambiguous set {RS, SC} → \"internacional rs\"; BRAS \"Internacional-RS\" → same ✓; bare LIB \"Internacional\" → dominance RS ✓; \"EC Internacional SC\" → designator strip ec → base internacional uf SC → \"internacional sc\" ✓; \"Internacional - SC\" (COPA?) ✓.\n\n   \"Sport Recife\" (BRF) → alias → \"sport\"; BRAS \"Sport-PE\" → base sport uf PE → sport ambiguous? UF variants of \"sport\": only PE → not ambiguous → drop → \"sport\" ✓✓.\n\n   \"EC Bahia\" → strip ec → bahia; \"Bahia de Feira\" separate base ✓.\n\n   \"Fortaleza EC\"/\"Fortaleza FC\" → fortaleza ✓ (only CE variant, dropped).\n\n   \"Guarani SP\" → base guarani uf SP; guarani ambiguous {CE,SP} → \"guarani sp\"; HIST bare \"Guarani\" → dominance → SP ✓ (HIST Guarani 2003 = Campinas-SP ✓). BRF \"Guarani de Juazeiro\" → separate base ✓. COPA \"Guarani - CE\" → \"guarani ce\" ✓.\n\n   \"Bragantino\" bare (BRF) → ambiguous {PA, SP} → dominance: count Bragantino-PA (COPA few) vs Bragantino-SP (BRF Serie B 2014-2019 many) → SP ✓.\n\n   \"America MG\"/\"America RN\" (BRF) → \"america mg\"/\"america rn\" ✓; COPA \"América - MG\"/\"América - RN\" ✓ same; FIFA \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → special alias → \"america mg\" ✓; FIFA \"América de Cali\"? Colombian — different base (\"america de cali\") — FIFA club, not match data; for player club join it just won't map to registry — fine. HIST bare \"América-MG\"? HIST 2012-2016 uses \"América-MG\" (suffixed) ✓.\n\n   \"Santa Cruz FC\" (BRF bare) → base santa cruz; ambiguous {PE,RN,RS} → dominance PE ✓ (Santa Cruz-PE the big one). \"Santa Cruz RN\"/\"Santa Cruz RS\" distinct ✓.\n\n   \"Nautico Capibaribe\" → alias → \"nautico pe\"; \"Nautico RR\" → \"nautico rr\" ✓; \"Nautico-PE\" → base nautico ambiguous {PE,RR} → \"nautico pe\" ✓ all consistent.\n\n   \"Operario\" variants {MS, MT, PR}: \"Operário-MS\" the Série B one → keys keep uf ✓.\n\n   \"Sao Paulo\": UFs? \"São Paulo-SP\" BRAS → base sao paulo uf SP → sao paulo ambiguous? only SP → drop → \"sao paulo\" ✓; bare ✓; \"Paulista Futebol Clube\" distinct base ✓; FIFA \"São Paulo FC\"? doesn't exist in this FIFA. fine.\n\n   \"Santos - AP\" → \"santos ap\" ✓; FIFA \"Santos\" bare → dominance → \"santos sp\" ✓ join with matches ✓; FIFA \"Santos Laguna\" → base \"santos laguna\" — NOT in match registry — player-club join unmapped, club search \"Santos\" must not match \"santos laguna\" → exact-key equality ✓.\n\n   \"Gremio Barueri - SP\" → base \"gremio barueri\" distinct ✓. \"Grêmio - RS\" → base gremio uf RS → gremio ambiguous? {RS} only? \"Gremio Esportivo Sapucaiense - RS\" is base \"gremio esportivo sapucaiense\" distinct. So gremio not ambiguous → \"gremio\" ✓ everywhere (BRAS \"Gremio-RS\", HIST \"Grêmio\", BRF \"Grêmio\"?? BRF list didn't show 'Gremio' in my big-club grep — it showed nothing for 'gremio fb'... hmm BRF \"Grêmio\"?? The grep list for BRF big clubs didn't include plain \"Gremio\" — odd! BRF team samples earlier: let me not worry; BRF certainly has Grêmio (Serie A 2014-2023). My grep filter had 'gremio fb' not 'gremio'... and the big-club filter DID include 'gremio fb' only. OK whatever — canonicalization handles both.\n\n   Also \"A.b.c. - RN\" → dots removed → \"a b c - rn\" → base \"a b c\" uf RN → hmm base \"a b c\" vs \"ABC - RN\" → \"abc - rn\"?? DOT REMOVAL: \"A.b.c.\" → replacing '.' with ' ' gives \"a b c \" vs \"ABC\" → \"abc\". MISMATCH! Fix: remove dots WITHOUT adding space: \"a.b.c.\" → \"abc\" ✓ (\"St. Pauli\"-style names rare here; \"A.b.c.\" is the case in point). But then \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo...)\" parenthetical. And names like \"C.R. Flamengo\"? not present. Dot-removal-no-space: \"a.b.c.\" → \"abc\" ✓ matches \"ABC\" ✓. \"AD Confianca\" fine.\n\n   What about COPA \"4 de Julho - PI\" vs BRF \"4 de Julho EC\" → base \"4 de julho\" ✓ (strip ec) → uf PI from copa; base unique → \"4 de julho\" ✓ join.\n\n   \"Aguia - PA\" (COPA?) vs \"Aguia de Maraba\" (BRF) → bases \"aguia\" vs \"aguia de maraba\" — different keys! Same club? Águia de Marabá-PA. COPA \"Aguia - PA\"... probably same club. Alias \"aguia\"→\"aguia de maraba\"?? But is there another \"Águia\"? \"Aguia Negra-MS\" is base \"aguia negra\" distinct. Bare \"aguia\" only with PA → I could alias \"aguia\" → \"aguia de maraba\". These small-club cases affect only cross-file dedupe of Copa↔BRF rows — Copa vs BRF overlap: dedupe only applies within same competition code (copa_do_brasil = COPA file + BRF \"Copa do Brasil\"). Imperfect small-club joins → a few duplicate rows in copa results 2014-2021 (both sources list same tie with slightly different team strings). How bad? Dedupe on (date, home_key, away_key) — date must match exactly too. BRF Copa do Brasil vs COPA file dates may differ anyway. Risk: inflated Copa match lists (duplicates shown twice). Mitigation: also dedupe Copa on (season, round-ish, home, away)? No — two-leg ties! Key (date, home, away) with date... if the two sources record the same leg on the same date but names canonicalize differently, dup. I'll measure: count copa matches per season after dedupe; expected ~120-160/season. If BRF+COPA both list same seasons with same dates → dedupe should collapse most. Check empirically after build.\n\n   For the big clubs (which is what queries/tests focus on), canonicalization is solid. For small clubs, imperfect but harmless (they appear in early Copa rounds).\n\n7. Display names: per key, most-frequent raw variant; prefer variants with accents (has_acccent bonus), prefer suffixed form for ambiguous keys. I'll compute: candidates sorted by (count, has_accent) desc; display = winner; then strip trailing \" - UF\"/\"-UF\" if key is non-ambiguous... hmm simpler: display keeps whatever the winning raw is, minus parentheticals. E.g. key \"flamengo\" winner \"Flamengo-RJ\" (BRAS 380×2... vs bare \"Flamengo\" HIST+LIB+BRF ~1168) → winner bare \"Flamengo\" ✓ nice. key \"palmeiras\": raws \"Palmeiras-SP\" (~400+) vs \"Palmeiras\" (~1083) → \"Palmeiras\" ✓. key \"atletico mg\": raws \"Atletico-MG\"(BRAS ~345×?) vs \"Atlético-MG\"(HIST) vs \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\"(copa few) vs \"Atletico Mineiro\"(BRF+FIFA) → counts: BRAS appearances ~ 2012-2022 with MG in most seasons (~345?) vs HIST 2003-2019 (~330) vs BRF 2014-2023 (~300+) vs FIFA 20 → tie-ish; prefer has_accent → \"Atlético-MG\" ✓ nice. key \"atletico pr\": raws \"Atletico-PR\"(BRAS), \"Athletico-PR\"(HIST 2019+COPA), \"Atlético-PR\"(HIST pre-2019, LIB), \"Atlético Paranaense - PR\"(COPA), \"Atletico Paranaense\"/\"Athletico Paranaense\"(BRF/FIFA), \"Athletico\"(LIB) → winner by count: BRAS \"Atletico-PR\" vs HIST \"Atlético-PR\"(2003-2018 ~16 seasons × ~34 = 544!) → \"Atlético-PR\" — with accent preference → \"Atlético-PR\" ✓ fine (display only).\n   \n   Display rule final: winner = max(raws, key=(count, has_accent, length)); display = winner. Simple, deterministic, good enough. For \"flamengo rj\"?? no — key \"flamengo\" is bare (only ambiguity {RJ, PI} → wait! flamengo UFs = {RJ, PI} → AMBIGUOUS → \"Flamengo-RJ\" → \"flamengo rj\"!! and bare \"Flamengo\" → dominance → \"flamengo rj\" ✓ same key — GOOD, they unify (that's the point). \"Flamengo - PI\" → \"flamengo pi\" separate ✓. Display for \"flamengo rj\": raws \"Flamengo-RJ\"(BRAS ~345), \"Flamengo\"(1168 bare), \"Flamengo - RJ\"(copa ~?), \"Flamengo do Piauí\"→no that's flamengo pi. → winner \"Flamengo\" (bare, most common) ✓✓ display \"Flamengo\" ✓.\n\n   So ambiguous bases: atletico{6}, america{2}, botafogo{2}, bragantino{2}, comercial{2}, flamengo{2}, guarani{2}, internacional{2}, juventude{2}, nautico{2}, operario{3}, rio branco{2}, santa cruz{3}, santos{2}, sao raimundo{2}, ypiranga{2}. (+ maybe more from BRF space-separated UFs like \"Coritiba PR\" — coritiba only PR → not ambiguous.)\n\n### Dedupe strategy (final)\nCompetition codes and sources:\n- `serie_a`: BRAS (2012-2022) + HIST (2003-2019) + BRF[tournament=Serie A] (2014-2023). Dedupe key (date, home_key, away_key). Expected post-dedupe per season: 2003:552, 2004:552, 2005:462, 2006-2022:380, 2023:377. If any season slightly >380 due to date mismatches between sources for same fixture — accept (they're rare) — but standings need \"complete-ish\" data; test 2019 exactly (BRAS vs HIST: do their dates match? earlier 2013 check: 342/380 common keys, 38 near-miss with NO date within 3 days — those 38 were the athletico spelling victims! With correct alias, recheck: should be ~380 common). Verify after build: 2019 standings must equal known truth (Flamengo 90). Also check per-season totals.\n- `copa_do_brasil`: COPA (2012-2021) + BRF[Copa do Brasil] (2014-2023). Dedupe same key. Imperfect small-club names → some residual dupes; acceptable. Round/stage text: COPA round number; BRF has no round column! BRF Copa rows lack round info — stage = \"\" for BRF. Dedupe keeps first-seen (priority: COPA first for round info? or BRF first for stats?) — for Copa matches, round info is MORE valuable (finals queries!). Keep COPA-priority for copa; keep BRAS-priority for serie_a? BRAS has round numbers for Serie A; HIST has Rodada too; BRF has stats. Priority for serie_a: BRAS (round+time) > HIST (round+arena) > BRF (corners). Hmm but corner stats only exist on the BRF copy. If we dedupe BRF away, corners are lost for 2014-2022 Serie A but kept 2023 (BRF-only season). Corners aren't in any required capability — \"Extended Match Statistics\" is a dataset that must be \"loadable and queryable\" though! Requirement: \"All 6 CSV files are loadable and queryable.\" Solution: keep BOTH a deduped \"primary\" match list AND preserve per-source stats: Match gets optional stats fields (corners, shots, attacks) — when deduping, MERGE stats from the dropped duplicate into the kept row? Cleanest: keep the deduped row from priority source, but if a dropped duplicate has stats, graft them onto the kept row. I'll implement merge-on-dedupe: stats (corners/shots/attacks/halftime) filled from any source that has them; venue from HIST; round from BRAS/HIST; kickoff from whichever. This makes each Match the richest union. \n- `libertadores`: LIB only. No dedupe needed (single source). 'NA' season row → season None.\n- `serie_b` / `serie_c`: BRF only.\n\nStandings: only for serie_a (and serie_b). Copa/Libertadores standings → error message explaining knockout format + offer finals. \n\n### queries.py API (final)\n```\nclass SoccerData:\n    matches, players, registry\n    find_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=None) -> list[Match]\n    last_match(team, opponent=None) -> Match | None\n    head_to_head(a, b, competition=None, season=None) -> dict with per-team wins/draws/losses + matches\n    team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue='all') -> TeamStats dict incl. home/away splits, gf, ga, win_rate\n    team_competitions(team) -> list of (competition, matches, seasons, first, last)\n    standings(competition='serie_a', season=None) -> list[TableRow]\n    competition_info(competition) -> summary dict\n    search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, limit=20, order='overall') -> list[Player]\n    club_overview(nationality='Brazil') -> list of (club_key, display, n_players, avg_overall) for Brazilian-club groups\n    avg_goals(competition=None, season=None) -> dict(avg, home_win_rate, draw_rate, away_win_rate, n)\n    biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, team=None, limit=10) -> list[Match]\n    best_records(venue='home', competition=None, season=None, min_matches=10) -> list[(team, stats)]\n    derbies(season=None, competition=None) -> list[(derby_name, [matches])]\n    brazilian_clubs() -> list of registry teams that are Brazilian (used by club_overview)\n```\n\nBrazilian-club detection for club_overview: registry teams from Brazilian competitions (all match files are Brazil-centric except Libertadores which includes foreigners — teams appearing in serie_a/serie_b/serie_c/copa (not libertadores-only) are Brazilian). Wait — Copa do Brasil includes only Brazilian clubs ✓. Serie A/B/C ✓. So Brazilian set = teams in {serie_a, serie_b, serie_c, copa_do_brasil} sources... careful: dedupe means source priority. Track per team: competitions played. Brazilian if plays in any of the 4 Brazilian competitions. Libertadores-only teams (Boca Juniors etc.) excluded ✓. But also: some Libertadores-only Brazilians? A Brazilian team that ONLY appears in LIB file... e.g. 2013-era teams all in serie_a too. Palmeiras etc. all covered. Fine.\n\nFIFA club → club_key join: canonicalize FIFA club string; if in registry → mapped. club_overview: group players with nationality=Brazil by club_key where club_key in Brazilian set.\n\n### formatting.py\nFormat functions mirroring the spec's answer formats:\n- format_matches_list(matches, title) with \"- 2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Round 22)\" style + \"... (N more matches in dataset)\"\n- format_h2h: \"Head-to-head in dataset: Flamengo 12 wins, Fluminense 8 wins, 7 draws\"\n- format_team_stats: the Corinthians example format\n- format_standings: numbered table + Champion marker + relegation zone marker\n- format_players: numbered list \"1. Neymar Jr - Overall: 92, Position: LW, Club: Paris Saint-Germain\"\n- format_club_overview: \"- Flamengo: 8 players (avg rating: 74)\" — only clubs actually in data; for Flamengo specifically: honest \"no Flamengo players in the FIFA snapshot; available Brazilian clubs: ...\"\n- format_stats: avg goals, home win rate\n- format_biggest_wins: \"1. 2012-05-27: Santos 8-0 Bolivar (Libertadores)\" wait Bolivar is Bolivian — \"Santos 8-0 Bolívar\" Libertadores 2012? hmm that's from spec example. biggest wins across competitions.\n- date formatting: ISO YYYY-MM-DD.\n\n### mcp_server.py — MCPServer with tools:\n```\nfind_team(name) → canonical resolution + alternatives\nsearch_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=30)\nhead_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)\nteam_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue='all')\nlast_match(team, opponent=None)\nteam_competitions(team)\nstandings(competition='serie_a', season=None)\nrelegation(competition='serie_a', season=None)\ncompetition_info(competition=None)\ncompetition_stats(competition=None, season=None)\nbiggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, team=None, limit=10)\nbest_records(venue='home', competition=None, season=None, min_matches=10)\nderbies(season=None, competition=None)\nsearch_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=20)\nclub_overview(nationality='Brazil')\ndata_summary()\n```\n17 tools. Competition parameter values: 'serie_a'|'serie_b'|'serie_c'|'copa_do_brasil'|'libertadores' (+ friendly aliases: 'brasileirao'→serie_a, 'brasileirão', 'copa'→copa_do_brasil, 'libertadores'). Normalize competition input.\n\nserver.py entry: \n```\nfrom brsoccer.mcp_server import main\nmain()  # server.run('stdio')\n```\n\n### Tests (BDD GWT)\nconftest: session-scoped `soccer_data` fixture (load once), `mcp` fixtures: in-process server + client session over stdio subprocess for integration.\n\nWait — stdio subprocess integration test: server startup loads all CSVs (~1-2s). OK.\n\nNumber the test scenarios to make \"At least 20 sample questions can be answered\" explicit — include a test that iterates 20+ representative questions through tools and asserts non-empty/expected answers (test_sample_questions.py) — directly maps to the success criterion.\n\nPerformance tests: simple lookup <2s, aggregate <5s (measure query layer on preloaded data; and one MCP round-trip after init).\n\n### pyproject.toml\n```\n[project] name=\"brsoccer-mcp\" version=\"2.0.0\" requires-python=\">=3.10\" dependencies=[\"mcp>=2.0\"]\n[project.scripts] brsoccer-mcp = \"brsoccer.mcp_server:main\"\n[tool.pytest.ini_options] addopts = \"-q\" testpaths=[\"tests\"]\n[build-system] setuptools\n```\nAlso requirements.txt (mcp, pytest, pytest-cov already in venv).\n\nBuild check: `pip install -e .` then `python -c \"import brsoccer\"`; entry point works. And run pytest.\n\nLet me now write all the files. Start with normalize.py, dates.py, models.py — then data.py, then a quick validation script (dedupe counts + 2019 standings) BEFORE writing queries/formatting/mcp/tests, so the foundation is verified early.\n\nActually, plan the todo list since this is a big task. Let me use todowrite. Then write files.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c6bd3e2001liCvvgMa0hstTv","sessionID":"ses_fa39bca00ffeV1iObla3wLMRAJ","messageID":"msg_05c6a1c1e0015234n5o79cskUK"}}
