y startswith base+\" \" or base startswith key?? e.g. query \"atletico mineiro\" → BASE_ALIASES handles → \"atletico mg\" ✓ exact. Query \"Boca Juniors\" → \"boca juniors\" exact ✓. Query \"atlético\" → bare ambiguous → dominance \"atletico mg\" — hmm as a QUERY it's ambiguous — resolve should return candidates ranked: for ambiguous-bare queries prefer returning ALL uf variants ranked by count rather than auto-picking. I'll implement: resolve returns list; key_of() used for indexing matches (deterministic); resolve() for query disambiguation returns: exact key hit first, then variants sharing the base sorted by match_count desc, then containment matches. The MCP find_team tool shows them all; other tools auto-pick the top resolution but append a note when multiple candidates.\n\nLet me now also define DERBIES:\n```python\nDERBIES = [\n    (\"Fla-Flu\", (\"flamengo rj\", \"fluminense rj\")),\n    (\"Clássico dos Milhões\", (\"flamengo rj\", \"vasco\")),\n    (\"Clássico Majestoso\", (\"corinthians\", \"palmeiras\")),\n    (\"Choque-Rei\", (\"corinthians\", \"sao paulo\")),\n    (\"San-São\", (\"santos sp\", \"sao paulo\")),\n    (\"GreNal\", (\"gremio\", \"internacional rs\")),\n    (\"Atletiba\", (\"atletico pr\", \"coritiba\")),\n    (\"Ba-Vi\", (\"bahia\", \"vitoria\")),\n    (\"Clássico Vovô\" — skip),\n]\n```\nCareful with keys: \"corinthians\" — base corinthians ufs {SP} → not ambiguous → key \"corinthians\" ✓. \"palmeiras\" ✓. \"sao paulo\" ✓. \"gremio\" ✓. \"bahia\" ✓. \"vitoria\" — depends on ambiguity analysis ({ES} only suffixed? if so key \"vitoria\" = BA club ✓; if {BA, ES} ambiguous → key \"vitoria ba\"!). DERBY defs must use FINAL computed keys — safer to resolve derby team names THROUGH the registry at query time (resolve(\"Bahia\") etc.). I'll define derbies with display names and resolve via registry at runtime. ✓ robust.\n\nTeamRegistry needs match_count per key + per competition for ranking/brazilian detection. The data loader will feed (raw, competition) occurrences; finalize computes keys, ambiguity, dominance, displays, and per-key competition sets.\n\nWrite normalize.py now. Also include `normalize_for_match(s)` used by player club search: FIFA club canonicalization for JOIN: uses same pipeline; if result key in registry → club_key; else club_key None but store normalized club string for search. For FIFA clubs not in match data, keep canonicalized string for equality search (\"real madrid\").\n\nPlayer club search semantics: query \"Real Madrid\" → norm \"real madrid\" → match players whose club_key == \"real madrid\" OR normalized club == query. Since FIFA club \"Real Madrid\" normalizes to \"real madrid\" ✓ exact. Query \"Flamengo\" → key \"flamengo rj\" — FIFA has NO Flamengo → zero hits via club_key; fallback containment: players whose raw club contains \"flamengo\"? none ✓ honest empty. Good.\n\nQuery \"Atlético Mineiro\" → \"atletico mg\" → FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" → \"atletico mg\" ✓ 20 hits.\n\nQuery \"Santos\" → \"santos sp\" (dominance) → FIFA \"Santos\" → \"santos sp\" ✓ 20 hits; FIFA \"Santos Laguna\" → \"santos laguna\" ≠ ✓ excluded.\n\nNow write the module.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c6dbb6a001WAP3UQOOktF9nt","sessionID":"ses_fa39bca00ffeV1iObla3wLMRAJ","messageID":"msg_05c6ceb9d001hYeaYuJkr2ImGG"}}
