 OK.\n\nNow the display `_pick_display` strips trailing \" -\" and leading/trailing hyphens via `.strip(\" -\")` — e.g. \"Palmeiras-SP\" → display \"Palmeiras-SP\"?? `_split_parenthetical(best)[0].strip(\" -\")` only strips spaces/hyphens at EDGES — \"Palmeiras-SP\" keeps internal hyphen → display \"Palmeiras-SP\". Hmm — but for key \"palmeiras\", the winning variant by count is bare \"Palmeiras\" (HIST+LIB+BRF ~1083 vs BRAS \"Palmeiras-SP\" ~400) → display \"Palmeiras\" ✓. For \"flamengo rj\" — winner bare \"Flamengo\" ✓. For \"atletico mg\": winner \"Atlético-MG\" (HIST ~330?) vs \"Atletico-MG\" (BRAS ~345?) vs \"Atletico Mineiro\" (BRF ~300 + FIFA 20) — close counts; whatever wins is a reasonable display (\"Atlético-MG\" if tie-break by accent). Display \"Atlético-MG\" fine.\n\nBut what about display for keys like \"santos sp\"? Winner: bare \"Santos\" (HIST 1136 + LIB + BRF) → display \"Santos\" ✓✓ (and \"santos ap\" winner \"Santos - AP\" → display \"Santos - AP\" → hmm ugly. `_pick_display` → best=\"Santos - AP\", split_parenthetical → same, strip(\" -\") → \"Santos - AP\". Meh — acceptable, or nicer: replace \" - \" with \"-\"? I'll normalize display dashes: `re.sub(r'\\s*[-–]\\s*', '-', ...)`? That'd render \"Santos-AP\" — clean. Add in _pick_display: collapse spaced hyphens to single \"-\".\n\nAlso `resolve()` containment fallback: `if query_norm and query_norm in entry_key` — for query \"palmeiras\" → base \"palmeiras\" already matched exactly. For \"Barcelona\" → LIB \"Barcelona-EQU\" → key? \"barcelona equ\"?? wait — \"Barcelona-EQU\" — uf extract: \"equ\" not a Brazilian UF → no uf → base \"barcelona equ\"?? Hmm — the token/regex only strips BRAZILIAN UFs, so \"Barcelona-EQU\" stays \"barcelona equ\". Ecuadorian suffix preserved in key — fine (distinct from FIFA \"FC Barcelona\" → \"fc barcelona\"→strip fc→\"barcelona\"). resolve(\"Barcelona\") → key_of(\"Barcelona\")=\"barcelona\" → no exact entry → base=\"barcelona\" → entry_keys == \"barcelona\"? No. startswith \"barcelona \"? \"barcelona equ\" ✓ matches → resolution found ✓ good enough.\n\nresolve() second phase: `entry_key == base or entry_key.startswith(base + \" \")` — query \"atletico\" (bare, ambiguous): key_of → dominant \"atletico mg\" exact ✓ + base \"atletico\" matches \"atletico ac/ba/es/go/mg/pr\" ✓ all listed. \n\nNow — FIFA club \"FC Barcelona\" → key_of → preprocess \"fc barcelona\" → strip designators? `key_of` pipeline: extract_uf(\"fc barcelona\") → no uf → base after _strip_designators... wait in key_of I do:\n```\nbase, uf = _extract_uf(plain)\nbase = _strip_designators(base)\n```\n→ \"fc barcelona\" → strip trailing? designators at edges: leading \"fc\"? TRAILING_DESIGNATORS stripped only from end; LEADING from start. \"fc\" is in TRAILING_DESIGNATORS — but it's LEADING here! \"FC Barcelona\" → leading \"fc\" NOT stripped (only \"ad\"/\"aa\" are leading). Result key \"fc barcelona\". Hmm — for player-club join that's fine (self-consistent: query \"FC Barcelona\" → \"fc barcelona\" matches). Query \"Barcelona\" → \"barcelona\" ≠ \"fc barcelona\" — resolve containment: \"barcelona\" in \"fc barcelona\" ✓ found. OK acceptable.\n\nWait, actually — should I strip leading \"ec\"/\"fc\" too? \"EC Bahia\" → leading ec! My _strip_designators: leading {ad, aa}, trailing {ec, fc}. \"EC Bahia\" → leading \"ec\" NOT stripped → base \"ec bahia\" ✗ — I wanted \"bahia\"! Fix: strip {ec, fc} from BOTH edges. Is that dangerous? A club named exactly \"EC...\"? no. \"EC\" leading: EC Bahia, EC Vitoria, EC Juventude, EC Internacional SC. So: LEADING = {ad, aa, ec, fc}, TRAILING = {ec, fc}. Hmm \"FC\" leading: \"FC Atlético Cearense\" → \"atletico cearense\" ✓ (matches COPA \"Atlético Cearense - CE\" → base \"atletico cearense\"... wait uf CE + base not ambiguous → \"atletico cearense\" ✓ join!). And \"FC Barcelona\" → \"barcelona\" — then join: FIFA \"FC Barcelona\" canonicalizes to \"barcelona\" — and LIB \"Barcelona-EQU\" is \"barcelona equ\" — DIFFERENT keys ✓ (Ecuadorian club ≠ FC Barcelona) and query \"Barcelona\" → \"barcelona\" → no registry entry (LIB club is \"barcelona equ\") → containment finds both ✓. \n\nHmm but wait: does FIFA club join matter for Barcelona? club_key is only set when the FIFA club key is in the match registry — \"barcelona\" not in registry (only \"barcelona equ\") → club_key None → fine.\n\nBUT danger: stripping leading \"fc\" — FIFA \"Fortaleza FC\" → trailing fc → \"fortaleza\" ✓ same as before. FIFA \"SC Braga\"?? leading \"sc\" — not stripped ✓ (sc not in designators) → \"sc braga\" — query \"Braga\" → containment ✓. OK.\n\nSo update LEADING_DESIGNATORS = {\"ad\", \"aa\", \"ec\", \"fc\"}. Hmm — \"AA\" e.g. \"AA Ponte Preta\"? Would strip to \"ponte preta\" ✓ good.\n\nOne more subtlety — `_strip_designators` loop: `while len(tokens) > 1 and tokens[0] in LEADING_DESIGNATORS` — for \"ec bahia\" → [\"bahia\"] ✓. For a club literally named \"EC\" alone (len==1) → preserved ✓.\n\nNow also think: BRF \"Santos AP\" → tokens [\"santos\",\"ap\"] → uf AP → \"santos ap\" ✓. COPA \"Santos - AP\" → same ✓.\n\nCOPA \"A.b.c. - RN\" → preprocess dots removed → \"abc - rn\" → extract → (\"abc\",\"RN\") → key \"abc rn\"?? base \"abc\" ambiguous? UFs for \"abc\": {RN} only → not ambiguous → key \"abc\"!! Hmm — \"Abc - RN\" → also \"abc\". So ABC-RN → key \"abc\" (suffix dropped since unique). And FIFA/other \"ABC\" → \"abc\" ✓ join. \n\nNow what about plain \"América de Cali\" (FIFA) → base \"america de cali\" — distinct key, not in registry (no match data) → club_key None. Fine.\n\nNext: `data.py`. Also fix the `__all__` typo and designators via edit. Then write data.py with:\n\n```python\nCOMPETITIONS = {\n \"serie_a\": \"Brasileirão Série A\",\n \"serie_b\": \"Brasileirão Série B\",\n \"serie_c\": \"Brasileirão Série C\",\n \"copa_do_brasil\": \"Copa do Brasil\",\n \"libertadores\": \"Copa Libertadores\",\n}\nCOMP_ALIASES = {\n \"brasileirao\": \"serie_a\", \"brasileirão\"→preprocessed, \"serie a\": \"serie_a\", \"a\": ?,\n \"copa\": \"copa_do_brasil\", \"copa do brasil\": ..., \"libertadores\": ..., \"copa libertadores\": ...,\n \"serie b\", \"serie c\", \"b\", \"c\"? keep simple: map normalized strings.\n}\n\ndef canonical_competition(text) -> code or None\n```\n\nLoader functions per file returning raw dicts; then a builder that:\n1. Pass A over match files: ingest all team raws into registry (counts).\n2. registry.finalize()\n3. Pass B: build Match objects with keys + displays; collect (competition, source_priority).\n4. Dedupe within competition by (date, home, away) — merge stats.\n5. Players: load fifa_data with utf-8-sig; club_key = registry key if in registry else normalized-string; store.\n\nDedupe priority for merged fields: keep first occurrence by priority order, but graft: stats (corners/shots/attacks) from any; venue from any non-None; kickoff from any; round/stage: prefer non-empty. Simplest: when duplicate found, merge into existing via dataclasses.replace. Match frozen — build a mutable accumulation then freeze at end. I'll implement with a small internal accumulator dict per key.\n\nPriority order per competition:\n- serie_a: sources ranked: \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\"(round+time) > \"novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv\"(round+arena) > \"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\"(stats)\n  Wait — which first matters only for fields present in multiple (date_text, stage). Merge semantics: first non-empty wins by priority. Stats only from BRF. Arena only from hist. OK.\n- copa_do_brasil: \"Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\" (round) > \"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\" (stats)\n- libertadores: single source\n- serie_b/serie_c: BRF only\n\nBRF stage: for Serie A/B/C BRF has no round → stage \"\" (or \"—\"). Hmm — BRF-only 2023 Serie A rows lack round numbers. Fine — stage \"\" → formatting handles.\n\nAlso BRF rows: parse floats \"1.0\" via parse_int ✓. date \"2023-09-24\" + time \"20:00:00\" separate columns — kickoff from time col; date from date col.\n\nHistoric: Data DD/MM/YYYY; Rodada → stage; Arena → venue; Vencedor ignored (recomputed); Ano → season.\n\nCopa: round \"1\"..\"8\" stage; datetime \"2012-03-07 16:00:00\".\n\nLibertadores: stage \"group stage\" etc; season may be \"NA\" → None; goals \"-\" → None.\n\nFIFA: ID, Name, Age, Nationality, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, Jersey Number, Height, Weight, Value, Wage + skills dict of a few (Crossing, Finishing, Dribbling, ...). Position may be \"\" (35 with empty position? those are probably free agents or GK variants — seen '' in positions list). Handle: position or None→\"\".\n\nClub: 241 empty → club \"\" → club_key None; club display \"Free agent\"? FIFA empty club usually means free agent. I'll keep club=\"\" and display \"Free agent\"? Hmm — search_players(club=...) shouldn't break. Keep raw \"\"; in formatting show \"Free agent\" if empty? Or just show \"No club\". Minor. I'll normalize display in formatting: club or \"Free agent\".\n\nPlayer skill dict: include a curated subset: Crossing, Finishing, ShortPassing, Dribbling, LongPassing, BallControl, SprintSpeed, ShotPower, Penalties, StandingTackle, SlidingTackle, GKDiving, GKReflexes, Interceptions, Vision, Composure, Strength, Stamina, HeadingAccuracy, Curve, FKAccuracy, Volleys, Aggression, Positioning, Marking, Reactions, Jumping, Agility, Balance, Acceleration, LongShots. These columns have \"84+2\" formats! e.g. \"88+2\". Need parsing: strip \"+N\" → int. Let me handle: value like \"88+2\" → 88. Or None if empty.\n\nAlso Value/Wage raw \"€110.5M\" keep as-is (display).\n\nStructure of SoccerData with indexes:\n```python\nclass SoccerData:\n    def __init__(self, matches, players, registry):\n        self.matches = sorted(matches, key=lambda m: (m.date or date.min, ...))\n        self.players = players\n        self.registry = registry\n        self._by_team: defaultdict(list)  # key -> [Match] (both sides)\n        self._by_comp_season: defaultdict(list)\n        self._players_by_club_key, _players_by_nat\n    @classmethod load_default(data_dir=None) -> SoccerData\n```\n\ndata_dir default: package-relative? The data lives in ./data/kaggle relative to repo root. Server may run from anywhere — resolve: env var BRSOCCER_DATA_DIR, else try (cwd)/data/kaggle, else module_path/../../data/kaggle? The package sits in repo root, so Path(__file__).parent.parent / \"data/kaggle\". Since installed editable, that works. Implement search: [env, cwd/data/kaggle, repo-relative]. \n\nNow write data.py. Also derby definitions — put in queries.py or normalize? I'll put DERBIES in queries.py using display names resolved via registry.\n\nLet me write the fixes to normalize.py first, then data.py.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c6f70cf001opG1hlqyoyaSie","sessionID":"ses_fa39bca00ffeV1iObla3wLMRAJ","messageID":"msg_05c6e775f001SpqJyM3U1ZjC0h"}}
