s not None}\n        if len(real) > 1: conflict += 1\n        elif len(real) == 1: agree += 1\n        else: partial += 1\n    per_season = defaultdict(set)\n    for (s,h,a) in buckets: per_season[s].add((h,a))\n    print(f\"{comp}: buckets={len(buckets)} multi={len(multi)} agree={agree} conflict={conflict} scoreless={partial}\")\n    sizes = {s: len(pairs) for s, pairs in sorted(per_season.items())}\n    print(\"   unique ordered pairs per season:\", sizes)\n\n# Copa: BRF vs COPA file overlap after season fix (cups keep date-year season)\nfor comp in ('copa_do_brasil',):\n    per = defaultdict(int)\n    copa_keys = {}\n    for r in raw:\n        if r['competition']!=comp: continue\n        per[(r['source'], r['season'])] += 1\n    print(f\"\\n{comp} rows by source/season:\", dict(sorted(per.items())))\n    # exact + fuzzy match analysis\n    brf = [(r['date'], reg.key_of(r['home_raw']), reg.key_of(r['away_raw']), r['home_goal'], r['away_goal'], r['season']) for r in raw if r['competition']==comp and r['source']=='BR-Football-Dataset.csv']\n    cup = [(r['date'], reg.key_of(r['home_raw']), reg.key_of(r['away_raw']), r['home_goal'], r['away_goal'], r['season']) for r in raw if r['competition']==comp and r['source']=='Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv']\n    cupset = {(d,h,a) for d,h,a,*_ in cup}\n    exact = sum(1 for d,h,a,*_ in brf if (d,h,a) in cupset)\n    fuzzy = 0\n    for d,h,a,hg,ag,s in brf:\n        if (d,h,a) in cupset: continue\n        for d2,h2,a2,*_ in cup:\n            if h==h2 and a==a2 and d2 and d and abs((d2-d).days) <= 1:\n                fuzzy += 1; break\n    print(f\"  BRF copa rows: {len(brf)}, exact-match w/ COPA file: {exact}, fuzzy(±1d same teams): {fuzzy}, unmatched: {len(brf)-exact-fuzzy}\")\nEOF","time":{"start":1788257367414,"end":1788257367423}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Excellent diagnosis! The problem is **date mismatches of one day** between sources for the same fixture (BRF dates are often +1 day vs BRAS — timezone recording difference), plus for 2021, BRF contains DIFFERENT matches (some early-2021 dates that don't exist in BRAS at all — BRF 2021 seems to mix in some Serie B rows?? \"coritiba vs palmeiras 2021-02-17\" — hmm 2021 season ran from May 2021... Feb 2021?? That's odd — BRF \"2021\" rows in Feb 2021 might actually be 2020-season postponed matches dated early 2021! The 2020 Brasileirão ended 2021-02-25 (COVID delay). So BRF rows dated Jan-Feb 2021 with season derived from date[:4] = \"2021\" are actually season-2020 matches!\n\nThat's a bug in MY BRF season derivation: I set season from the date year. For BRF, the date year ≈ season except for these Jan/Feb boundary rows (2020 season spilling into 2021; also 2021 season... wait BRF rows Feb 2021 = tail of 2020 season → season should be 2020). Similarly BRF 2015 Serie A 378 rows etc.\n\nFixes needed:\n\n1. **Fuzzy date dedupe**: same fixture recorded ±1 day across sources. For serie_a, each (season, home, away) pair plays exactly twice (home/away legs). Dedupe key should be more tolerant: (season, home, away) appears at most 2 times per season in a league! For serie_a/serie_b/serie_c I can dedupe on (season, home, away) allowing up to 2 rows — but the THIRD occurrence would be a duplicate... but with date-graded selection. Cleaner approach: **dedupe on (date, home, away) EXACT, then a second pass merging rows with same (competition, season, home, away, home_goal, away_goal) within a ±1 day window** — same teams, same score, adjacent date = same match recorded with a timezone-shifted date. For 2021 BRF weirdness: \"internacional rs vs gremio 2021-01-24 goals 2-1\" vs BRAS \"internacional vs gremio 2021-11-06\" — DIFFERENT dates (not ±1) and different scores — these are the TWO DIFFERENT LEGS of the same season pair! (Round 1 in Jan? no...) Hold on — 2021 season: BRAS has Inter vs Grêmio on 2021-11-06. BRF has Inter vs Grêmio 2021-01-24?? 2021 Série A started May 29, 2021. A Jan 2021 match between Inter and Grêmio = the 2020 season's fixture (2020 season ended Feb 2021; Inter vs Grêmio round 30ish in Jan 2021 plausible). So BRF row is a 2020-season match mis-dated as season 2021 by my date[:4] derivation. My season derivation from date is wrong for Jan-Feb rows.\n\n   BRF \"2021\" Feb rows (coritiba vs palmeiras 2021-02-17): 2020 season round (Coritiba-Palmeiras Feb 2021 ✓ real: they played 2021-02-17 in the 2020 Brasileirão!). And BRAS 2020 has coritiba vs palmeiras 2020-?? hmm BRAS 2020 season rows: does BRAS file date the 2020-season matches in Feb 2021? BRAS 2020: 380 rows — the Brasileirão 2020 season file records season=2020 with dates possibly extending into 2021. Let me check: BRAS rows with season=2020 — max date? If BRAS has coritiba-palmeiras on 2021-02-17 too, then my BRF date[:4]→2021 assignment splits it. FIX: for BRF, season = max(date.year, ...) hmm no — correct approach: **BRF rows in Jan/Feb belong to the PREVIOUS calendar year's season** (Brazilian seasons run ~May-Dec, except pandemic-extended 2020 ending Feb 2021). Rule: for BRF (and generally matches in Jan-Apr), season = date.year - 1 if month <= 4 else date.year. Check: are there any genuine Brazilian seasons starting before May? 2020 season started Aug 2020 (pandemic), ended Feb 2021. Normal seasons: May-Dec. Some early rounds in April (e.g., 2019 started April 28?). Hmm April 28 2019 — month<=4 → season 2018 ✗ WRONG. Risky.\n   \n   Safer rule: month <= 2 (Jan/Feb) → previous year. A genuine season match in Jan/Feb: only the pandemic 2020 tail. 2012-2023 Serie A: any Jan/Feb matches that are NOT the 2020 tail? Seasons normally end by Dec 20-ish. So month<=2 → year-1 is safe for these datasets. March/April: seasons can start late April — keep same year ✓ (2019 started Apr 28? hmm actually 2019 round 1 was April 28? BRAS 2019 first match: 2019-04-27ish — fine, month 4 → same year ✓).\n\n   BUT this only applies to leagues (serie_a/b/c) and copa? Copa do Brasil runs Feb-Nov (first rounds in Feb!). Copa 2014-2023 from BRF — first round Feb 2014 → season = date.year-1 = 2013 ✗ WRONG. So: apply the Jan/Feb→prev-year shift ONLY to league competitions (serie_a/b/c). Copa/Libertadores start Feb-Apr; their Jan/Feb matches (if any) belong to same year... Copa first round mid-Feb usually — same year ✓. Libertadores starts Feb/Mar ✓ same year. So: leagues: month<=2 → year-1; cups: year.\n\n   Hmm wait — one more: are there BRF Serie A rows in Jan/Feb OTHER than the 2020 tail? E.g. \"2023-01-xx\" rows from the 2022 season tail? 2022 season ended Nov 2022 (post-pandemic normal). BRF date range max 2023-12-07, min 2014-05-21. Check for Jan/Feb rows: 2021-01/02 (2020 tail) and maybe 2021-02 rows from both 2020 tail. Also 2015-01/02? 2014 season ended Dec 2014 ✓. So the rule handles exactly the 2020-tail rows. But also — do I even NEED BRF season precision? Yes for dedupe keys and season queries.\n\n   And note: BRAS 2020 rows — their dates: do they extend to Feb 2021 with season=2020? BRAS file season column says 2020 ✓ regardless of date. HIST doesn't cover 2020. So the 2020-tail BRF rows get season 2020 after fix, dedupe against BRAS 2020 rows on exact date? BRAS coritiba-palmeiras 2020-season row: dated 2021-02-17? If BRAS also dates it 2021-02-17 ✓ exact dedupe works.\n\n2. **The ±1 day date mismatches** (2019 BRF: 81 rows off by one day vs BRAS/HIST): after fixing season, still need date-tolerant dedupe. Approach: dedupe key for leagues on (competition, season, home, away) — in a league each pair meets exactly twice; I can collect rows per (comp, season, home, away) and cluster by date adjacency + score. But BRF 2021 also showed rows with same teams, DIFFERENT dates (Jan vs Nov — different legs) — that's the two legitimate legs (each team hosts once). Per (comp, season, home, away) — home and away fixed — Inter HOME vs Grêmio away: BRF has 2021-01-24 (should be 2020-season after fix → different season → separate ✓) and BRAS 2021-11-06 — after season fix they don't collide ✓. So within (comp, season, home, away) there are normally just 1-2 matches (league double round-robin: each ordered pair meets once per half... wait — (home=A, away=B) ordered pair meets exactly ONCE per season in double round-robin! A hosts B once, B hosts A once. So key (comp, season, home, away) has EXACTLY ONE legitimate match for leagues!). \n   \n   So for LEAGUES: dedupe on (comp, season, home, away) — dates don't even matter! Much more robust. Two rows with same ordered pair in a season = duplicate recording (possibly with slightly different dates/scores from different sources). Merge them (prefer priority source's score/date).\n   \n   For CUPS (copa_do_brasil, libertadores): teams can meet multiple times per season (two-legged ties, group+knockout), so ordered-pair key is WRONG. Dedupe on exact (date, home, away) + ±1-day fuzzy merge: cluster rows by (season, home, away, home_goal, away_goal) where dates within 1 day → same match. Actually simpler: exact (date, home, away) key, then merge pairs with same (season, home, away, score) and |Δdate| <= 1. For copa BRF-vs-COPA-file mismatches: are their dates off by one too? BRF Copa do Brasil 2014-2021 vs COPA 2012-2021... earlier union issue: copa 2014-2021 double-listed? Let me check counts after implementing.\n\n   Wait — actually for leagues, is (season, home, away) ALWAYS unique-legit? 2003-2005 had 46+ rounds... still double round-robin: ordered pair meets once. 2021 BRF 491 rows → after season-fix how many unique (season, home, away)? If BRF 2021 has BOTH legs of a pair with home/away same order... no. Let me just implement league dedupe on (comp, season, home, away) and verify counts: every season should be ≤ 380 (or ≤ number of teams-appropriate) and 2019 standings should equal known truth.\n\n   Edge: what if two sources list same ordered pair with DIFFERENT scores (data conflict)? Keep priority source's (BRAS > HIST > BRF for serie_a). Fine.\n\n   Edge: season missing (None) rows (LIB 'NA') — for cups use exact-date key fallback.\n\n3. **BRAS vs HIST 2019: 2 rows each unmatched** — dates differ by 1 day between the two files (vasco-cruzeiro Dec 2 vs Dec 1; csa-gremio Jul 29 vs 28). League ordered-pair dedupe fixes these too ✓.\n\n4. Also there might be a residual issue: BRF rows for the same ordered pair in a season that are LEGITIMATELY distinct — impossible in league double round-robin. Unless... playoffs? Serie C 2014-2023: Serie C has a final phase (quadrangular) — teams meet MORE than twice, and (home, away) ordered pair could repeat?? Serie C format: 20-ish teams, group stage + final hexagon. In the final phase, teams from same group meet again — ordered pair CAN repeat within a season! E.g. Serie C final quadrangular: A vs B, B vs A, and if same pair from group stage... group stage A-home-B once, B-home-A once; quadrangular: A-home-B AGAIN possible! So Serie C ordered pairs CAN legitimately repeat. Serie B: pure double round-robin (38 rounds) ✓ unique. Serie A ✓ unique.\n\n   So: league dedupe on (season, home, away) is safe for serie_a, serie_b (verify counts), but serie_c needs date-aware dedupe: exact (date, home, away) + ±1-day same-score merge. Let me check serie_c structure: 2023 Serie C 215 rows... Serie C has ~20 teams... whatever — use the cup-style dedupe for serie_c (date-based fuzzy), and ordered-pair dedupe for serie_a/serie_b only.\n\n   Actually, even simpler UNIFIED approach: fuzzy-merge everywhere:\n   - Pass 1: bucket by (comp, season, home, away).\n   - Pass 2: within bucket, cluster rows: same match if same score AND |Δdate| <= 1 day. Different score or far dates → separate rows.\n   - For serie_a/serie_b this collapses the ±1day/same-score dupes; BRF-vs-BRAS conflicting scores would remain separate — do those exist? For 2021 after season fix, BRF rows are all same-season-2021 with BRAS... if scores conflict they'd stay as two rows → season count >380 → standings corrupted. Hmm.\n   \n   Let me check empirically: after the season fix, for serie_a, per (season, home, away) bucket how many rows have conflicting scores vs agreeing. If agreeing (mod score-None), pure bucket-collapse works for a/b. Let me diagnose first, then choose.\n\nAlso check copa overlap between BRF Copa and COPA file (dates off-by-one? team spellings for small clubs?).\n\nLet me run the diagnosis.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c71ccde001vOc8zJ6iJGDDKI","sessionID":"ses_fa39bca00ffeV1iObla3wLMRAJ","messageID":"msg_05c711412001etOmtPBoF2dQcJ"}}
