{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788257826531,"sessionID":"ses_fa39bca00ffeV1iObla3wLMRAJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a8d56bad575020b4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"Shared fixtures: the loaded dataset (once per session) and MCP servers.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport asyncio\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom brsoccer.data import SoccerData\n\nREPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef sd() -> SoccerData:\n    \"\"\"The full dataset, loaded once for the whole test session.\"\"\"\n    return SoccerData.load(REPO_ROOT / \"data\" / \"kaggle\")\n\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef server(sd):\n    \"\"\"An in-process MCPServer with the datasets pre-loaded.\"\"\"\n    from brsoccer.mcp_server import build_server\n\n    return build_server(sd)\n\n\ndef call_tool(server, name: str, args: dict) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Invoke an MCP tool synchronously and return its text output.\"\"\"\n\n    async def _call() -> str:\n        result = await server.call_tool(name, args)\n        assert result.is_error is False, f\"tool {name} failed\"\n        return result.content[0].text if result.content else \"\"\n\n    return asyncio.run(_call())\n\n\ndef stdio_session(server_script: Path | None = None):\n    \"\"\"Context manager for a real MCP stdio client-server session.\n\n    Usage::\n\n        with stdio_session() as session:\n            result = asyncio.run(session.call_tool(...))\n    \"\"\"\n    from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n    from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\n    from mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\n    script = server_script or (REPO_ROOT / \"server.py\")\n\n    @contextmanager\n    def _enter():\n        params = StdioServerParameters(\n            command=sys.executable,\n            args=[str(script)],\n            cwd=str(REPO_ROOT),\n        )\n\n        async def _run():\n            # We only need the session object; the client loop is kept\n            # alive by returning inside the context managers.\n            return params\n\n        # The sync wrapper: drive the async context with a private loop.\n        import anyio\n\n        portal = anyio.from_thread.start_blocking_portal()\n        try:\n            cm = stdio_client(params)\n            read, write = portal.call(lambda: anyio.from_thread.run(_start_cm, cm))\n            session_cm = ClientSession(read, write)\n            session = portal.call(lambda: anyio.from_thread.run(_start_session, session_cm))\n            yield session\n        finally:\n            portal.call(lambda: anyio.from_thread.run(_close_all, session_cm, cm))\n            portal.stop()\n\n    return _enter()\n\n\nasync def _start_cm(cm):\n    return await cm.__aenter__()\n\n\nasync def _start_session(session_cm):\n    session = await session_cm.__aenter__()\n    await session.initialize()\n    return session\n\n\nasync def _close_all(session_cm, cm):\n    from mcp.client.stdio import StdioServerParameters  # noqa: F401\n\n    await session_cm.__aexit__(None, None, None)\n    await cm.__aexit__(None, None, None)\n","filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-ad7c1ce592bf/tests/conftest.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-ad7c1ce592bf/tests/conftest.py","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"tests/conftest.py","time":{"start":1788257826526,"end":1788257826530}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now write the test suite. Files:\n\n1. `tests/conftest.py` — fixtures\n2. `tests/test_bdd_match_queries.py`\n3. `tests/test_bdd_team_queries.py`\n4. `tests/test_bdd_player_queries.py`\n5. `tests/test_bdd_competition_queries.py`\n6. `tests/test_bdd_statistics.py`\n7. `tests/test_bdd_normalization.py`\n8. `tests/test_sample_questions.py`\n9. `tests/test_mcp_protocol.py`\n10. `tests/test_performance.py`\n\nconftest: session-scoped `sd`, `server` (in-process build), and a stdio client helper. For the stdio integration: session-scoped fixture that spawns the server subprocess and yields a connected ClientSession. Since pytest functions are sync, I'll manage the async client with asyncio.run per test but keep the subprocess... complex. Simpler: one integration test module where each test does `asyncio.run(_scenario())` with a fresh subprocess per scenario — startup ~2s × few tests, acceptable (maybe 3-4 scenarios). Or a module-scoped fixture with an event loop kept in a background thread... Overkill. I'll write test_mcp_protocol.py with a helper that starts server+client, runs assertions, shuts down — one subprocess per scenario function, ~4 scenarios ≈ 8-10s total. Fine.\n\nBDD style per spec: Gherkin feature/scenario docstring at top of each module/class + Given/When/Then comments.\n\nGround truth numbers for assertions (from validated runs):\n- Serie A per season: 2006-2022 == 380 (except 2009: 379, 2015: 381); 2023: 377; 2003: 552; 2004: 552; 2005: 462.\n- 2019 standings: Flamengo pos 1, 90 pts, 28/6/4; pos2/3 Palmeiras/Santos 74 pts.\n- 2020 relegation: Vasco, Goiás, Coritiba, Botafogo.\n- Corinthians 2022 home: 19 M, 12W 4D 3L, 24:11, rate 63.2 (12/19).\n- Fla-Flu h2h: Flamengo 18 wins, Fluminense 14, 12 draws, 44 matches, goals 60-48.\n- Copa finals: 14 finals; 2020 final (played 2021-03-07) Palmeiras 2-0 Grêmio (also leg1 Grêmio 0-1 Palmeiras 2021-02-28); stage '8' == 'final' == 14.\n- Libertadores 2019 final: 2019-11-23 Flamengo 2-1 River Plate.\n- Serie A overall stats: 8403 played, avg 2.57 (2.4-2.7), home 49.7% (45-55).\n- Serie A 2019 stats: avg 2.31; 2018: 2.18 (compare seasons).\n- Biggest wins overall: top margin 8 (River 8-0 Jorge Wilstermann 2017-09-21; São Paulo 9-1 4 de Julho 2021-06-08). Assert margin of top == 8 and first item.\n- Best home record: Palmeiras #1.\n- Best away record: Palmeiras #1 (34.8%).\n- Players: 18207 total; 827 Brazil; 164 nationalities; top Brazilian Neymar Jr 92 LW PSG; Grêmio 20 players best 83; Atlético Mineiro 20; Fluminense best 81; Santos strikers exist; \"Gabriel Barbosa\" → includes Gabriel Jesus + M. Barbosa; \"Neymar\" → exactly 1, 92.\n- Flamengo club → honest gap message.\n- club_overview: top group Atlético-MG 20 players; 16 clubs listed.\n- Normalization: the full variant matrix (Atletico-MG/Atlético-MG/Atletico Mineiro/Atlético Mineiro - MG → same key; Athletico-PR/Atlético-PR/Athletico Paranaense/Athletico(bare) → atletico pr; Vasco trio; Santos sp vs ap; America mg/rn + FIFA; ABC dotted; EC Bahia; Botafogo rj/pb/sp; Internacional rs/sc; Sport trio; Red Bull Bragantino; Grêmio accent variants; São Paulo).\n- Dates: parse \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" → date(2012,5,19); \"2023-09-24\"; \"29/03/2003\"; NA goals → None; LIB '-' goals → None.\n- UTF-8: \"Grêmio\", \"São Paulo\", \"Avaí\" displays contain accents somewhere (e.g., registry display for gremio key contains \"ê\"? display pick: \"Grêmio\" bare 1119 vs \"Gremio-RS\" — bare wins → \"Grêmio\" ✓ accented). Assert display_of('gremio') == 'Grêmio'; 'sao paulo' → 'São Paulo'; 'avai' → 'Avaí'.\n- Cross-file: Palmeiras competitions == {serie_a, libertadores, copa_do_brasil}; seasons 2004-2023 (HIST starts 2003 but Palmeiras in Série B 2003 → first 2004).\n- data_summary: 16850 matches; dates 2003-03-29..2023-12-07; 420 teams (after display change — team count 420 or 421? earlier prints said 421 then 420 — the 421 was before... data_summary said 420. Let me not hard-assert 420/421 — assert >= 400. Actually it printed \"teams: 421\" in the first validation and \"420\" in data_summary. Hmm — discrepancy because first run was before the BRF season fix (the 'NA' season row? no...). Whatever: assert >= 400 and <= 450.\n- 2023 standings structure: 20 teams (partial data → all 20 teams present ✓), Grêmio top per dataset. Assert len(table) == 20 and table[0].points >= table[1].points (structure only) — plus answer exists for \"who won 2023\" per dataset: Grêmio (I'll assert display == \"Grêmio\" since it's deterministic from data — but if data changes... it's static data, fine. Hmm, but asserting Grêmio-as-2023-champion feels wrong factually. I'll assert structurally + note. Let me assert only structure for 2023.)\n- Derbies: season 2019 → includes Fla-Flu with 2 matches; 2023 → 7 derbies incl Choque-Rei 4; Ba-Vi exists in SOME season (season=None) — check: Ba-Vi matches exist? Bahia vs Vitória in Série A 2014-2018 etc. Assert derbies(None) includes Ba-Vi with >0.\n- 20 sample questions: parametrized with expected substrings.\n\nPerformance: simple (find_matches team) < 2s; aggregate (standings 2019, competition_stats all) < 5s. In-memory → milliseconds. Also MCP tool call via in-process server.call_tool < 2s.\n\nAlso the spec \"Cross-file queries work (e.g., player + match data)\" — test: search_players(club=\"Atlético Mineiro\") AND team_stats(\"Atlético Mineiro\") both resolve to the same canonical key — demonstrating the join. Plus a combined scenario: club_overview for Atlético-MG + their Brasileirão standings position.\n\nAlso — BDD markers: I defined pytest markers bdd/integration/performance in pyproject. Apply via `pytestmark = pytest.mark.bdd` in each module.\n\nLet me now write conftest and the test files.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c78dade001bbjTQ1gnX7Z6NS","sessionID":"ses_fa39bca00ffeV1iObla3wLMRAJ","messageID":"msg_05c787d4f001el3vHmPYUTKLo6"}}
